(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"Love-And-Duty-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Price of Being the Avatar","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"Aang missed our dinner.\n\nThat sentence should not have mattered as much as it did.\n\nThe city was negotiating control of the eastern rail corridor. Two neighborhoods had threatened to block construction. A council member from the Fire Nation delegation had resigned. Aang had spent the week moving from meeting to meeting with the sort of patience that looked effortless until I watched him come home too tired to finish a bowl of soup.\n\nWe all knew his schedule was impossible.\n\nThe day before the missed dinner, I went to the rail office to find out who had actually asked for Aang.\n\nA clerk handed me a stack of requisitions with the resignation notice on top. Three neighborhood delegates had requested a hearing about whose homes the eastern line would cut through. The council had placed the Avatar's name on the agenda as mediator, witness, and final approving authority. None of the delegates had asked him to approve anything.\n\n“Who put that last job in?” I asked.\n\nThe clerk frowned. “It's what we always do. The Avatar has to keep the peace.”\n\n“Keeping the peace isn't the same as signing away somebody's courtyard.”\n\nShe stiffened. “I didn't write the proposal.”\n\n“I know. I'm asking who did.”\n\nAt home I showed Katara the agenda. She read the addresses, then the names, then the space where the residents' own representative should have been listed.\n\n“They invited the wrong person to speak for them,” she said.\n\n“Again.”\n\nToph took the paper and held it against the table with two fingers. “They could've invited the people who live there. Revolutionary idea.”\n\nThe next day Aang arrived home long after the dinner we had planned. He had spent two hours trying to keep a council member from accusing the delegates of disloyalty. When I told him about the empty representative line, he looked as angry as I'd seen him all week.\n\n“I thought they asked for me,” he said.\n\n“They asked for somebody to listen. You were the name the council wrote down.”\n\nHis eyes closed. “I should've checked.”\n\n“Yeah,” I said. “Next time. Tonight I still wanted dinner with you.”\n\nThat was harder than explaining the paperwork. He looked at the cold bowls, then at me, and didn't try to make what he'd missed disappear with another promise about saving the world.\n\nWe planned dinner anyway.\n\nNot a public appearance.\n\nNot a strategy session.\n\nNot food eaten over maps.\n\nDinner.\n\nKatara cooked. I brought sweet buns. Toph showed up with a bottle of plum wine and claimed this counted as emotional labor.\n\nAang promised to be home by seven.\n\nAt eight, the food was cold.\n\nAt nine, Katara reheated it.\n\nAt ten, Toph opened the wine.\n\nAt eleven, I stopped pretending I was not angry.\n\n“He could have sent a messenger.”\n\nKatara sat across from me with both hands around her cup.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe agreement surprised me.\n\nI had expected her to defend him.\n\nToph stretched out on the floor. “He screwed up.”\n\n“I know he’s busy.”\n\n“That doesn’t make it not a screwup.”\n\nI looked at Katara.\n\nShe seemed tired in a different way than clinic-tired.\n\n“This has happened before,” she said.\n\nThe anger in me changed.\n\n“With you?”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n“Not this exact dinner. But… the Avatar always has somewhere else he could be. Someone else who needs him. Aang hates disappointing people, so sometimes he says yes to everyone until the person he disappoints is the one who already loves him.”\n\nThat hurt because I understood it.\n\nI also hated it.\n\nToph said, “He does it because he knows we won’t start a war if he’s late.”\n\nKatara’s mouth twitched.\n\n“I have considered it.”\n\nThe door opened at eleven thirty.\n\nAang stepped inside looking exhausted and immediately stopped.\n\nThe three of us were still awake.\n\nThe table was still set.\n\nHis face fell.\n\n“Oh.”\n\nToph sat up. “Strong opening.”\n\nAang looked at the clock.\n\nThen at us.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nKatara asked, “What happened?”\n\n“The rail delegation split. Then Minister Shinu arrived early and—”\n\nI stood.\n\nAang stopped.\n\n“I don’t need the reason first,” I said.\n\nHe looked confused.\n\n“I need you to understand what happened here before you explain what happened there.”\n\nSilence.\n\nAang set down his staff.\n\n“You waited.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I should have sent word.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I thought I’d be done in another few minutes.”\n\n“For four hours?”\n\nHe winced.\n\nKatara remained quiet.\n\nToph’s face was unreadable.\n\nAang rubbed both hands over his face. “I’m sorry.”\n\nThe apology sounded sincere.\n\nIt was not enough yet.\n\n“I believe you.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“I’m still angry.”\n\nAang’s shoulders tightened.\n\nThere it was: the instinct to make the feeling go away.\n\nI saw him catch it.\n\nHe breathed.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nThat one word mattered more than another apology would have.\n\nI sat again.\n\nAang took the empty cushion.\n\nKatara finally spoke.\n\n“I need something too.”\n\nHe turned toward her.\n\n“I need you to stop assuming I’ll understand because I always have.”\n\nAang’s face crumpled slightly.\n\n“Katara—”\n\n“No. Listen.”\n\nHe did.\n\n“I know what your work is. I know people can die when you make the wrong choice. I know sometimes you genuinely cannot leave.”\n\nShe leaned forward.\n\n“But I also know you sometimes stay because leaving feels selfish, even when the crisis has enough people without you.”\n\nAang looked down.\n\nToph said, “And you like being needed.”\n\nThe room sharpened.\n\nAang’s head snapped toward her.\n\n“That’s not fair.”\n\nToph did not move.\n\n“Isn’t it?”\n\nHis voice rose. “I don’t do this because I enjoy people depending on me.”\n\n“I didn’t say enjoy.”\n\n“You said I like it.”\n\n“I said being needed feels familiar.”\n\nThat silenced him.\n\nToph’s tone softened, just barely.\n\n“You woke up and the whole world needed you. Maybe sometimes going home feels like abandoning something because nobody’s screaming.”\n\nAang stared at the floor.\n\nKatara’s anger shifted into sadness.\n\nI watched him understand himself in real time.\n\n“That’s awful,” he whispered.\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“It’s a pattern. Not a moral verdict.”\n\nAang laughed once without humor. “You all planned this?”\n\n“No,” Katara said.\n\nToph added, “If we planned it, there’d be snacks.”\n\n“There are sweet buns,” I said.\n\n“Cold sweet buns.”\n\nAang’s mouth twitched.\n\nThe tension broke enough for breathing.\n\nWe made a plan.\n\nIf Aang would be more than thirty minutes late, he sent a messenger unless someone was literally on fire.\n\nIf a meeting had enough competent people without him, he practiced leaving.\n\nIf he needed to stay, he told us rather than disappearing into duty.\n\nAnd if we were angry, he was not allowed to fix the anger by immediately becoming adorable.\n\n“That feels targeted,” he said.\n\n“It is,” Katara replied.\n\nWe ate cold dinner after midnight.\n\nThe food was not good anymore.\n\nThe conversation was.\n\nWhen Toph went to bed, she punched Aang lightly in the shoulder.\n\n“Nice job being emotionally trainable.”\n\n“Thank you?”\n\nKatara kissed his cheek and started clearing dishes.\n\nAang caught her wrist.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“For more than tonight.”\n\nKatara’s face softened.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThey hugged.\n\nI watched without feeling outside it.\n\nThen Aang came to me.\n\nHe stopped close but did not touch.\n\n“Can I?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe wrapped his arms around me.\n\nI let myself be angry and love him at the same time.\n\nThat was another thing I was learning.\n\nLove did not require feelings to arrive one at a time.\n\n“I’m still annoyed,” I murmured into his shoulder.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You have to be up in six hours.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Your orange robe is stained with ink.”\n\n“I—what?”\n\nI pulled back.\n\nKatara started laughing.\n\nAang looked down at the black line across his sleeve.\n\nToph shouted from the hall, “Administrative Companion strikes again!”\n\nAang groaned.\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nThe price of loving the Avatar was that the world would always ask for him.\n\nThe price of being loved by us, apparently, was that sometimes he had to say no.\n\nNeither price was small.\n\nBoth were real.\n\nThe next morning, Aang tried to apologize for being gone by offering to make breakfast.\n\nThis was a tactical error.\n\nHe could cook in the broad sense that food entered a pan and eventually became edible. Katara watched him attack a cabbage with a knife and took the blade away before the meal required healing.\n\n“I wanted to do something for you,” he said.\n\n“You can wash the rice,” she replied.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“Do not involve me. I value my life.”\n\nToph, already eating fruit she had stolen from the counter, said, “I’ll eat whatever survives.”\n\nThe room loosened around the joke, but the missed dinner still sat with us.\n\nAang washed the rice.\n\nAfter a while he said, “When people need the Avatar, it feels wrong to think about what I want.”\n\nKatara’s hands stopped over the cutting board.\n\n“That is exactly when you have to,” she said.\n\nHe looked at her.\n\n“Wanting something doesn’t mean you choose it every time,” she continued. “But if you stop noticing what you want, duty gets to make every decision before you even arrive.”\n\nToph crunched into an apple. “And then you get weird.”\n\n“I’m already weird.”\n\n“Weirder.”\n\nI sat across from him. “What did you want last night?”\n\nHis answer took too long.\n\n“To be here.”\n\nNobody told him he should have been.\n\nThat would have turned love into another duty.\n\nInstead, I said, “Then next time, tell us that too. Even if you still go.”\n\nAang nodded.\n\nThe rice boiled over.\n\nToph pointed toward the stove. “Avatar crisis.”\n\nAang lunged for the pot while the rest of us laughed.\n\nThat evening Aang sent a messenger before leaving for another dispute. The note contained only six words: I want to stay. I’m going.\n\nKatara read it twice.\n\nToph tapped the paper and said, “Better.”\n\nI folded it into my pocket. He came home after midnight. Nobody pretended the absence had not mattered, and nobody punished him for answering a call he believed he had to answer.\n\nWe made room for both truths at once. It was harder than choosing one and calling it virtue.\n\nA month later, the pattern changed again. Aang came home early from one dispute because another mediator could finish the work. He looked almost guilty about it until Katara asked whether the town was safe. He said yes. Toph asked whether he had been necessary. He hesitated, then said no.\n\n“Congratulations,” she said. “You discovered delegation.”\n\nAang dropped into the chair beside me. “I hate how obvious you make everything sound.”\n\n“That’s because I’m wise.”\n\n“You’re twenty-one.”\n\n“Ancient.”\n\nHe laughed and stole food from my plate only after I nodded. The world had survived without every sacrifice he could possibly offer it. For one evening, he let that be good news.\n\nHe came home tired, but he came home by choice.\n","word_count":1809},"Love-And-Duty-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Festival That Wasn’t There","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Aang woke us before sunrise by ringing a bell in the courtyard.\n\nToph threw a pillow through the window.\n\nThe bell stopped.\n\nAang leaned around the doorframe five seconds later, hair windblown and expression deeply offended.\n\n“That was a ceremonial bell.”\n\n“It was a loud ceremonial bell.”\n\n“It belonged to the Eastern Air Temple.”\n\nToph sat upright in bed. “Then why was it in my courtyard?”\n\n“Our courtyard,” Katara mumbled into the pillow beside her.\n\nToph pointed toward the sound of her voice. “Traitor.”\n\nI opened one eye. “Why are we awake?”\n\nAang brightened.\n\nThat was never a good sign before dawn.\n\n“Yangchen’s Festival.”\n\nToph fell backward.\n\nKatara pushed herself up on one elbow. “Today?”\n\n“Traditionally, yes.”\n\n“You said next week.”\n\n“I was wrong.”\n\nToph’s voice came muffled through the blanket. “Avatar wisdom.”\n\nBy breakfast, the house was full of strings, cloth, prayer wheels, flour, fruit, and three Air Acolytes who had arrived carrying a crate of things Aang insisted were not decorations.\n\n“They have symbolic purpose.”\n\n“They’re ribbons,” Toph said.\n\n“They can be both.”\n\nThe festival had not been celebrated properly in more than a century.\n\nThat fact lived underneath everything Aang did that morning.\n\nHe smiled. He gave instructions. He tied colored strips of cloth from the roofline and corrected an Acolyte’s pronunciation gently enough that the young man did not notice he had been corrected at all.\n\nBut every few minutes Aang stopped.\n\nHis hand would rest on some ordinary object—a carved bowl, a length of saffron cloth, a bundle of incense—and his expression would go distant.\n\nMemory without memory.\n\nThat was one of the cruelest things about what had been taken from him. He could remember his people. He could remember individual monks, games, rules he broke, meals he hated, the way certain halls smelled when it rained.\n\nBut he had been twelve.\n\nThere were ceremonies he had watched without learning. Songs he knew only in fragments. Customs adults would have explained later, if later had happened.\n\nNow people looked at him as if being the last Air Nomad meant he must also be the complete archive of them.\n\nAt midmorning, he handed me a stack of copied festival notes.\n\n“Can you organize these?”\n\nI flipped through them. “By what?”\n\nAang frowned.\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHalf came from his own memory. Some had been copied from temple records. Others came from old Earth Kingdom travel diaries written by visitors who had watched Air Nomad festivals from outside and misunderstood half of what they saw.\n\nOne paper claimed the holiday required floating three onions down a river.\n\nAang stared at it.\n\n“I have never heard of that.”\n\nToph took the page. “Keep it.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nKatara carried trays of dough into the courtyard. “What did the festival actually celebrate?”\n\nAang’s face softened.\n\n“Gratitude. Letting go of what we don’t need. Remembering that everything changes.”\n\nToph bit into a piece of uncooked dough.\n\nKatara slapped her hand away.\n\n“That part seems useful,” I said.\n\nAang looked toward the street beyond the gate.\n\nThe city had changed even in the months I had known it. New smokestacks interrupted the horizon. Construction cranes turned above old tile roofs. People from every nation filled streets that had once belonged to one empire or another.\n\n“Yeah,” he said. “It does.”\n\nWe held the festival at the edge of town, on a low hill where an abandoned Air Nomad waystation had once stood.\n\nOnly the foundation remained.\n\nAang had found it in an old map and spent weeks getting the council to protect the site from a factory expansion. Toph said the stones were still good. Katara found a spring nearby and cleared the channel until water ran again.\n\nI made signs explaining that visitors should not climb on the ruin.\n\nNobody read them.\n\nBefore the gathering began, an elderly neighbor asked whether the ribbons were supposed to carry the names of the dead. Aang hesitated. He had imagined releasing burdens, not creating a memorial roll.\n\n\"I should have explained,\" he said.\n\n\"You explained what it means to you,\" Katara replied. \"They may need something different.\"\n\nToph planted a heel in the dirt. \"Also, your writing table is crooked. The left leg sank.\"\n\nShe lifted the ground beneath it just enough to level the brushes. Aang set blank paper beside the instructions.\n\n\"I can't claim this is exactly how my people celebrated,\" he told the first guests. \"Some of it I remember. Some of it we're making together. You can write, draw, or leave the paper empty.\"\n\nOne person wrote a brother's name. A child drew a tree.\n\nKatara checked where the loose ribbons would land. \"We have to collect the ones that fall into the stream. I don't want somebody else's water spoiled by our ceremony.\"\n\n\"I can help them rise,\" Aang said.\n\n\"And I can make sure they don't stay in the creek.\"\n\nToph brought four cups of water. \"And I can make sure you noble idiots drink something.\"\n\nWhen I laughed, she bumped my shoulder. The festival was imperfect. It was better than pretending history had left us flawless directions.\n\nThe gathering grew larger than expected.\n\nAir Acolytes came in gray and yellow robes. Local families arrived out of curiosity. Children chased paper gliders down the hill. Vendors appeared because vendors could smell a crowd across three districts.\n\nAang tried to object to the fried-dough cart.\n\nThen he ate two.\n\nAt noon, he stood on the old foundation and spoke.\n\nNot as the Avatar.\n\nAs Aang.\n\n“This used to be a small stop for travelers,” he said. “Not a temple. Not a sacred place because somebody important lived here. Sacred because people rested here. Shared food. Told stories. Then moved on.”\n\nThe crowd quieted.\n\nHe held one of the copied pages in his hand.\n\n“I don’t know everything about this festival.”\n\nA few Acolytes looked surprised.\n\nAang smiled a little.\n\n“I know some things. I remember some. I’ve learned others from records. And some things are gone.”\n\nThe last word landed harder than the rest.\n\nKatara moved closer to him.\n\nToph stayed where she was, feet planted on the stone.\n\nAang continued. “If we pretend I remember what I don’t, then we aren’t preserving Air Nomad culture. We’re inventing certainty because grief makes uncertainty uncomfortable.”\n\nI looked at him and felt my throat tighten.\n\n“That doesn’t mean we do nothing,” he said. “It means we practice honestly. We say what we know. We say what we don’t. We keep the values that mattered: compassion, freedom, joy, nonviolence, care for all life. And we let living people learn how to carry them forward.”\n\nThe wind rose as he spoke, tugging at ribbons overhead.\n\nFor one breath, the hill seemed crowded with ghosts.\n\nNot visible.\n\nNot supernatural.\n\nJust present in the space Aang made for them without pretending they could answer every question.\n\nLater, we tied written burdens to thin paper streamers and let them go from the ridge.\n\nNot an exact historical ritual, Aang warned everyone.\n\n“A new one,” he said.\n\nToph held her paper for a long time.\n\n“What’d you write?” I asked.\n\n“None of your business.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nShe crumpled it.\n\nThen smoothed it again.\n\nAang stood several paces away helping a child fold a glider. Katara was laughing with an elderly woman who had mistaken rice flour for face powder and somehow covered both of them in white dust.\n\nToph angled her head toward me.\n\n“You?”\n\nI looked down at my own strip.\n\nI had written: THE FEAR THAT I AM BORROWING SOMEONE ELSE’S FAMILY.\n\n“Also none of your business.”\n\n“Good answer.”\n\nWe walked to the ridge together.\n\nToph did not need to see the horizon to know where the wind was coming from. She lifted her paper and waited until the current pulled against it.\n\n“On three?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nShe let go immediately.\n\nI laughed and released mine after hers.\n\nThe papers spun upward.\n\nToph could not follow them with her eyes.\n\nI almost described it.\n\nThen I stopped myself.\n\n“Want to know?”\n\nHer mouth tilted.\n\n“Yeah.”\n\n“Yours got caught in an updraft. Mine’s losing a fight with a tree.”\n\n“Sounds right.”\n\nAang came up behind us.\n\n“Toph, did you actually write something?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re a terrible liar.”\n\n“I’m the best liar. I can tell when everybody else is doing it.”\n\n“That is not how that works.”\n\nKatara joined us and slipped an arm around my waist.\n\n“What did you write?” I asked her.\n\nShe looked toward the streamers.\n\n“That I don’t have to keep every version of home exactly the way I remember it.”\n\nAang turned toward her.\n\nKatara gave him a small smile.\n\n“The South is changing. This city is changing. We’re changing.”\n\nToph made a gagging noise.\n\nKatara leaned over and kissed her cheek.\n\nToph froze.\n\nThen, very deliberately, wiped her cheek on my shoulder.\n\n“Gross.”\n\n“You’re blushing,” Aang said.\n\n“I will bury you in this sacred historical site.”\n\n“You can’t. Paperwork put up a sign.”\n\nI pointed downhill. “Finally, respect for municipal authority.”\n\nThat evening, after the crowd left, we stayed to clean.\n\nThe hill was littered with cups, snapped strings, crumbs, and the evidence of three hundred people attempting spirituality near food vendors.\n\nAang swept with a broom instead of bending.\n\nI asked why.\n\nHe shrugged. “Monks made us clean after festivals.”\n\n“Tradition?”\n\n“Punishment.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Being children near a festival.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe leaned on the broom.\n\n“Was it wrong?” he asked.\n\nI knew what he meant.\n\nThe new ritual. The things he had filled in. The celebration built partly from memory and partly from choice.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“No,” I said. “I know you told the truth about what you knew. You didn’t ask anyone to confuse invention with history.”\n\nHis gaze dropped.\n\n“And it made people care enough to learn more.”\n\nAang looked across the hill where an Air Acolyte carefully wrapped an old stone marker before carrying it indoors for preservation.\n\nThe wind moved through his robes.\n\n“Maybe that’s enough for now.”\n\n“Maybe ‘for now’ is allowed.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen he hooked the broom behind my knees with airbending and almost knocked me into a basket.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“Sacred tradition?”\n\n“Extremely.”\n\nI chased the Avatar down the hill while Toph shouted unhelpful tactical advice and Katara laughed hard enough to drop the trash bag.\n\nBehind us, new ribbons snapped in the evening wind above old stones.\n\nNot restoration.\n\nNot replacement.\n\nSomething living.\n","word_count":1736},"Love-And-Duty-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Vote","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"We lost by one vote.\n\nAfter months of hearings, petitions, neighborhood meetings, revised language, public arguments, private arguments, and one memorable session where Toph threatened to bend a councilman’s chair into a smaller chair, the Civic Balance Charter failed seven to six.\n\nThe chamber erupted.\n\nSupporters shouted.\n\nOpponents cheered.\n\nMara Sen said a word that caused three reporters to stop pretending not to listen.\n\nI sat at the clerk’s table and stared at the tally.\n\nSeven.\n\nSix.\n\nOne vote.\n\nThe charter would have created enforceable anti-discrimination rules for city employment and housing, independent safety standards for bending and nonbending workers, public conflict-of-interest logs, and elected neighborhood councils with reserved nonbender representation where bending professions dominated local government.\n\nIt was not perfect.\n\nThat was part of why losing hurt.\n\nWe had compromised until every sentence had fingerprints on it.\n\nThe deciding vote belonged to Councilor Chen.\n\nOf course it did.\n\nHe had spent six weeks describing himself as undecided and two days before the vote received a large campaign donation from an industrial association that opposed the worker-safety provisions.\n\nLegal.\n\nTechnically.\n\nI hated technically.\n\nAfter the chamber emptied, a textile worker named Suri approached the clerk's table. Her hands were stained indigo, and she'd worn the same patched coat to every hearing. She had argued for the nonbender safety provisions so consistently that I recognized the scrape of her shoes on the tile before she spoke.\n\n“I want a copy of the roll call,” she said.\n\n“The votes are public,” the clerk answered.\n\n“Not the speeches. The votes. I want my neighbors to know who actually stood up for the rule about fireproof exits.”\n\nI pushed the tally sheet toward her. Her fingertip rested on Chen's name for a moment, then moved down the column.\n\n“People will tell us we lost because we asked for too much,” she said. “I thought it wasn't too much to want to reach the door before a furnace explodes.”\n\n“It isn't.”\n\n“Then don't come back with a version where we have to give that up just to get six votes.”\n\nI had been drafting concessions in my head since the result was called. The pages in my folder seemed to grow heavier.\n\n“You might not like our next proposal,” I said.\n\n“I might not. You're supposed to bring it to us before you ask me to sell it to the workshop.”\n\nThat hurt more than Chen's smug smile had. She was right. I'd started thinking of my neighbors as a constituency I needed to reassure instead of adults who could disagree with the strategy.\n\nI wrote down her name and the workshop's requested meeting time, then stopped myself.\n\n“May I give the committee your contact?”\n\n“No. Tell them the union steward will send a representative. I've got a shift tomorrow.”\n\nShe took her copy of the votes and went. I sat with the ink drying under my hand, thinking of every time I'd told Aang he didn't get to decide the future for other people. Apparently the lesson was not reserved for the Avatar.\n\nAang stood in the public gallery.\n\nHe had refused to use Avatar authority to pressure the council.\n\nThat was correct.\n\nI hated that too.\n\nKatara sat beside him, jaw tight.\n\nToph was not allowed in the chamber after the chair incident.\n\nShe waited outside.\n\nThe council adjourned.\n\nPeople poured into the hall.\n\nReporters surrounded Aang immediately.\n\n“Avatar, will you override the vote?”\n\n“Do you believe the council has failed nonbenders?”\n\n“Will you withdraw support from Councilor Chen?”\n\nAang’s face went still in the public way.\n\n“No,” he said. “The charter was a civil legislative process. I supported it publicly, but I will not turn disagreement into a reason for the Avatar to replace elected government.”\n\nA reporter pushed closer. “Even when the result harms balance?”\n\nAang’s expression sharpened.\n\n“Balance is not me getting every outcome I want.”\n\nI heard the sentence.\n\nI understood it.\n\nI wanted to scream.\n\nMara caught my arm before I walked away.\n\n“Don’t disappear.”\n\n“I’m going outside.”\n\n“You’re going home to write a better charter at two in the morning.”\n\n“That is slander.”\n\n“It’s prophecy.”\n\n“I’m not the Avatar.”\n\n“Thank every spirit available.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nThen I nearly cried.\n\nMara’s grip softened.\n\n“Losing is part of politics.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“There you go knowing things again.”\n\nI glared.\n\nShe released me.\n\n“Come to the meeting tomorrow.”\n\n“What meeting?”\n\n“The one where we decide what we do next.”\n\nI stared at her.\n\n“You scheduled it already?”\n\n“I expected either victory problems or defeat problems.”\n\nI loved her a little.\n\nOutside, Toph leaned against the courthouse wall.\n\n“Well?”\n\nI did not need to answer.\n\nShe could hear the crowd.\n\n“Seven-six,” I said.\n\nToph’s mouth flattened.\n\n“Chen?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Chair.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Tiny chair.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Stool?”\n\n“Toph.”\n\nShe kicked the wall.\n\nA small crack appeared.\n\n“Property damage,” I said automatically.\n\n“Put it on my tab.”\n\nKatara emerged next.\n\nShe looked furious enough to freeze the rain.\n\nAang followed, surrounded by aides.\n\nOne handed him another schedule.\n\nAang looked at it.\n\nThen tore it in half.\n\nEveryone stopped.\n\nThe aide stared.\n\nAang said, “I’m going home.”\n\nIt was a tiny rebellion.\n\nI loved it.\n\nAt home, nobody spoke for the first ten minutes.\n\nToph opened the bottle she had bought for the housing hearing months ago.\n\nKatara made tea because she said we were not drinking on empty stomachs.\n\nAang sat on the kitchen floor with his back against a cabinet.\n\nI put the vote tally on the table.\n\nSeven.\n\nSix.\n\nToph tapped the paper.\n\n“So we change one.”\n\nKatara sat across from me. “Or the language.”\n\nAang rubbed both hands over his face.\n\n“Or the public.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe lowered his hands.\n\n“Chen can take money because voters don’t punish him for it.”\n\n“That’s cynical,” Katara said.\n\n“It’s also true,” I said.\n\nAang sighed.\n\n“I’m trying to be less naive.”\n\nToph pointed at him. “Don’t overcorrect. You’ll get boring.”\n\nHe threw a towel at her.\n\nShe caught it.\n\nI stared at the tally.\n\n“I thought if the argument was good enough…”\n\nMara’s voice echoed in my head.\n\nLosing is part of politics.\n\nKatara said, “Arguments matter. So does power.”\n\nAang looked at her.\n\nShe continued. “Who has money. Who has time to attend meetings. Who gets believed. Who is afraid of losing work if they sign a petition.”\n\nToph added, “Who owns the building the meeting happens in.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\n“That was almost profound.”\n\n“Shut up.”\n\nWe spent the evening not solving it.\n\nThat was harder than solving things.\n\nAang kept reaching for possibilities and putting them back down because each one crossed some line of influence he did not want to cross.\n\nKatara wanted accountability immediately and hated that due process was slow.\n\nToph wanted direct pressure and hated any system that rewarded politeness from people with less power.\n\nI wanted to rewrite the text until language could defeat money.\n\nNone of us got what we wanted.\n\nAround midnight, I stood to get my folders.\n\nThree voices said, “No.”\n\nI stopped.\n\n“This is different.”\n\nToph threw the towel again.\n\n“It’s midnight, Paperwork.”\n\n“We just lost.”\n\n“Yeah,” Katara said. “We lost.”\n\nShe came around the table.\n\n“And you are not going to turn losing into punishment.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\nAang’s expression softened.\n\n“No penance by paperwork.”\n\n“That phrase is offensive.”\n\n“It’s good,” Toph said.\n\n“Betrayal.”\n\nKatara took my hand.\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\nI did.\n\nBadly.\n\nAt three in the morning, I woke and found Aang sitting by the window.\n\n“So much for rest,” I whispered.\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n“I was trying.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nThe city outside was dark except for factory lamps and a few late windows.\n\n“Do you regret not intervening?” I asked.\n\nAang took a long time to answer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThat surprised me.\n\n“And no.”\n\n“Very helpful.”\n\n“I could have pressured Chen.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“People would probably praise me for it.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And then the next Avatar might pressure a council for something worse.”\n\nI leaned my head against the wall.\n\n“Duty is annoying.”\n\nAang laughed quietly.\n\n“I thought you liked rules.”\n\n“I like rules other people follow.”\n\nToph’s voice came from the hallway.\n\n“I heard that.”\n\nWe both jumped.\n\nShe walked in carrying a blanket.\n\nKatara followed with three more.\n\n“You’re all terrible at sleeping,” she said.\n\n“We were having a constitutional discussion,” Aang said.\n\n“At three in the morning.”\n\n“Democracy never rests.”\n\nToph groaned. “Kill it.”\n\nKatara draped a blanket over all of us.\n\nWe sat by the window until dawn.\n\nWe had lost.\n\nNo hidden victory.\n\nNo miraculous reversal.\n\nJust one vote and the work after it.\n\nWhen sunlight reached the factory roofs, I finally understood why the defeat mattered to the story we were building.\n\nLove did not guarantee justice.\n\nGood arguments did not guarantee votes.\n\nPower used carefully was still power.\n\nDuty sometimes meant refusing the shortcut even when the shortcut led somewhere you desperately wanted to go.\n\nAt nine, I went to Mara’s meeting.\n\nI left my pen at home on purpose.\n\nToph brought me another one.\n\nMara’s meeting took place in a bakery after closing.\n\nNobody had reserved a hall. Nobody had an agenda printed on official paper. Someone wrote discussion points on the back of flour invoices and passed them around with leftover rolls.\n\nI listened.\n\nThat was harder than speaking.\n\nA machinist wanted the charter split into smaller ordinances so opponents could not kill every reform with one vote. A teacher argued that doing so would let the council approve symbolic pieces while avoiding the parts that redistributed actual authority. Two shop owners disagreed about whether another public hearing would help or merely repeat the same speeches.\n\nNo one asked me to solve it.\n\nEventually Mara looked over. “You’ve been quiet.”\n\n“I’m practicing.”\n\n“Painful?”\n\n“Excruciating.”\n\nThe room laughed.\n\nI unfolded my hands. “The council knows how to defeat our current proposal. So we stop giving them the same proposal.”\n\nThat started another hour of argument.\n\nGood.\n\nWhen I came home, Toph was asleep on the couch with a pen tucked behind one ear. Aang had fallen asleep on the floor beside a stack of cultural-school applications. Katara was still awake, reading by the stove.\n\n“How was it?” she asked.\n\n“Messy.”\n\nHer smile said she understood.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n“We might lose again.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That doesn’t make tonight pointless?”\n\n“No.”\n\nI leaned my head against her shoulder.\n\nAcross the room, Toph muttered without opening her eyes, “Told you.”\n\n“You were asleep.”\n\n“Still right.”\n\nThe first vote had taught me something ugly and useful: losing did not automatically mean the work had failed. Sometimes it meant the strategy had.\n\nThe next morning, my borrowed pen was still on the table. I returned it to Toph.\n\nShe pushed it back.\n\n“Keep it.”\n\n“For luck?”\n\n“Don’t be ridiculous.”\n\nI kept it anyway.\n\nI used that pen at the next meeting. We still argued. This time, losing was no longer the only ending I could imagine.\n","word_count":1828},"love-and-duty-42":{"number":42,"title":"The Shape of Fair Time","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"Our family scheduling argument began with a square drawn in blue ink.\n\nIt was supposed to represent Tuesday.\n\nKatara had divided the page into days, with each day divided into morning, afternoon, and evening. She had written clinic shifts in neat columns. Aang's council meetings occupied the top half of the week. Toph's bridge inspections ran along the bottom in a handwriting large enough to be considered structural reinforcement.\n\nMy own commitments were squeezed into the margins.\n\n\"That's not Tuesday,\" I said.\n\nKatara examined the square.\n\n\"It is literally Tuesday.\"\n\n\"It looks like a punishment.\"\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Give me the calendar. I'll earthbend us a new week.\"\n\n\"We cannot bend time,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Then we stop pretending Tuesday can hold four people and the whole city,\" I said.\n\nKatara tapped the clinic column. \"These are patients. They don't disappear because I have a date.\"\n\n\"Nor should they. But you can ask which shifts someone assumed you'd cover for free.\"\n\nToph jabbed her finger at the bridge inspection. \"Mine happens in daylight. Bad bolts don't schedule around romance.\"\n\nAang reread a council request. \"They want me to settle the crossing dispute.\"\n\n\"Who asked? The neighbors or the secretary?\" I said.\n\nHe studied the signature. \"The secretary.\"\n\n\"There's the Avatar-shaped hole everybody throws work into,\" Toph said.\n\nAang wrote a question instead of an acceptance. Katara agreed to ask another qualified healer whether they wanted a paid shift. I put my Starwake errands in their own column and reminded everyone that my preference for polyamory did not turn every hour of my life into shared property.\n\nToph tipped back her chair. \"Fine. Now we just need someone to invent a second Tuesday.\"\n\nAang brightened. \"I could—\"\n\n\"No,\" Katara and I said together.\n\nFor once he listened before trying to make the world more convenient.\n\n\"Then why are you the Avatar?\"\n\nHe gave her the patient look he used when he was trying very hard to remember that he loved her.\n\nI had agreed to the meeting because the previous week had been a mess. I was supposed to help Iroh at the pantry on Wednesday, check the supply ledger at Brasswatch on Thursday, and spend Friday evening with Katara, just the two of us. Instead, the bridge inspection ran late, Danse needed an additional sample, and I forgot to tell Katara that I'd promised to review a different set of measurements.\n\nBy the time I arrived home Friday, there were two cold bowls on the table and no one in the kitchen.\n\nKatara hadn't shouted.\n\nThat was worse.\n\nNow she sat across from me with her hands folded, waiting.\n\n\"Tell me what bothered you,\" I said.\n\n\"I waited,\" she answered. \"Then I stopped waiting because I had an early shift. I don't mind that you help people. I mind finding out you won't be home from a note you leave after I've already made plans.\"\n\nI stared at the calendar.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nAang looked relieved.\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"That was fast. Usually people spend an hour defending themselves first.\"\n\n\"I can still do that if you want entertainment.\"\n\n\"No thanks.\"\n\nKatara looked at me, not smiling.\n\n\"I know our relationships are complicated. I know we agreed we weren't going to measure affection by equal hours. But when we make plans, I need to be able to trust them.\"\n\n\"I agree.\"\n\nAang cleared his throat.\n\n\"I think I've done something similar.\"\n\nToph turned toward him.\n\n\"Only the part where you promise you'll be home by sundown and then spend three hours negotiating with a council because you don't want anybody upset.\"\n\nAang winced.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\n\"You're all painfully reasonable today.\"\n\n\"Give us time,\" I said.\n\nThe difficulty wasn't that we had too many people to love. It was that we kept mistaking good intentions for a working system. Nobody wanted to appear selfish. So we waited until the disappointment hurt enough to become an argument.\n\nKatara pushed the blue square toward me.\n\n\"Tell me where you actually need time this week.\"\n\n\"Monday evening with you, if you want it. Wednesday I need a quiet night by myself. Thursday I'm with Iroh for the pantry inventory. Saturday I promised Greg I'd help set up the pier.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"And when do we spend time together?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Do you mean everyone, or you and me?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I meant everyone. But maybe I want both.\"\n\n\"Then we should write both.\"\n\nToph tapped the edge of the table.\n\n\"And stop treating the whole group as one giant person.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" Katara said.\n\nToph leaned back, pleased with herself.\n\nI wrote separate lines for individual time and group time. It looked less tidy. It was more honest.\n\nWe found out quickly that fairness was not the same as giving everyone an identical share of every week. Katara needed reliable advance notice because of her clinic shifts. Aang wanted space for spontaneous visits without making them obligatory. Toph preferred a whole afternoon of work and teasing to an evening of ceremonious romance. I needed time alone and time for other people I cared about. None of those needs were defects.\n\nThen Toph made a noise like she'd swallowed a nail.\n\n\"What?\" I asked.\n\n\"You've left a space for me on Friday.\"\n\n\"Do you want it?\"\n\n\"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Then it stays open. No obligation.\"\n\nShe turned her head toward me.\n\n\"You don't have to keep a chair for someone who hasn't said yes.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to offer.\"\n\nFor a moment she looked almost shy.\n\n\"Maybe,\" she said.\n\nI nearly fell off my chair.\n\nAang smiled into his tea.\n\nKatara caught him.\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm not doing anything.\"\n\n\"You're glowing.\"\n\n\"Airbender condition.\"\n\nToph threw a napkin at him.\n\nThe calendar looked tidy enough to be suspicious.\n\nBefore I could congratulate us, Toph tapped the corner of the page.\n\n“That's not a calendar,” she said. “That's a trap with very tidy boxes.”\n\nKatara frowned. “We've left free time.”\n\n“Free for whom? My bridge review starts at dawn. If I spend Tuesday night with you, I get four hours of sleep and then somebody asks me why the west supports are crooked.”\n\nAang looked at the row he'd marked for himself. He had set aside three consecutive evenings for council negotiations. He hadn't included the time he'd need to prepare.\n\n“I thought I could finish early,” he said.\n\nToph made a derisive noise. “I thought I could teach the entire city to earthbend by lunchtime. We're both disappointed.”\n\nI moved my own commitments out of the margin and onto a separate sheet. One belonged to Greg in Beach City, another to Danse at Brasswatch, another to a quiet evening I wanted entirely alone. The fact that Jasper was the person who traveled between the doors did not make the whole set of obligations flexible.\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous,” I told them, “and I prefer polyamory. You know that. But a calendar doesn't turn all the people I love into one giant relationship with interchangeable hours.”\n\nKatara nodded. “And being together doesn't mean we stop needing time apart.”\n\nAang took the brush. “Then we should put *rest* on the page first.”\n\n“And no assigning *my* rest,” Toph added.\n\nShe drummed her heel against the floorboards while I marked my writing time. I was surprised at how relieved I felt seeing an empty square stay empty. Nobody filled it with a favor.\n\nWhen the final version was ready, we agreed to try it for a week. A schedule could be an invitation to keep promises; it was not permission to make them for someone else.\n\nI counted the squares once more, then realized I'd put three people's needs on Wednesday evening and left Thursday almost empty. It had seemed fair when I was filling in boxes. It looked less fair when I imagined actual people trying to get through a door.\n\n\"Can we test one week before calling this finished?\" I asked.\n\nToph leaned back. \"The schedule has homework now?\"\n\n\"It always did,\" Katara said.\n\nAang agreed to handle Appa's morning care on the days Katara had clinic appointments. Toph wanted the bridge review kept separate from household chores, because having a gift for stone didn't mean she was available to fix every broken shelf. I wrote down my writing time and asked everyone not to treat it as an empty square simply because I would be home.\n\n\"How do we tell when somebody's overwhelmed?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Ask them,\" Toph said. \"Revolutionary concept.\"\n\nKatara laughed. \"And believe the answer.\"\n\nWe tried a mock change: a clinic message arriving during a planned family dinner. Katara would decide whether she needed to go. Aang would check whether transport was required without volunteering Appa before discussing the return. Toph would not be drafted to babysit anyone's emotional reaction, and I would be allowed to say I was disappointed without making the disappointment an argument.\n\nThe first attempt was awful. We all talked at once.\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said, \"we should practice one person speaking at a time.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Not a chance.\"\n\nBut she waited for me to finish. Aang went next, then Katara. The conversation took longer, and everyone understood more of it.\n\nI thought about how long it had taken us to name the relationship as polyamorous, about telling each partner plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred love that could be shared honestly. A calendar couldn't do the loving for us. It could stop ordinary mistakes from becoming proof that somebody was unwanted.\n\nBy supper, the plan was imperfect but usable. Katara leaned against my shoulder while Aang cleared the table. Toph stole the last dumpling and claimed it was part of the test.\n\n\"Of what?\" I asked.\n\n\"Whether you can survive disappointment.\"\n\n\"Apparently I can.\"\n\nShe smiled in the particular way that warned me she was pleased, then kicked my chair gently under the table. It was nothing like a formal vow. It felt more like a family.\n\nWe finished the calendar in an hour, which was astonishing considering how often Toph tried to rename Wednesday. We made one rule I cared about particularly: changing a plan meant actually telling the people affected. No silent assumptions that a loving partner would understand without being asked.\n\nThen we discovered the missing problem.\n\nThe house had only one quiet room.\n\nAang used it to meditate. Katara used it to prepare clinic records. Toph went there when too much noise made it hard to concentrate on what she felt through the floor. I went there to write.\n\nAll four of us had been politely avoiding it when someone else was inside.\n\n\"Which means,\" I said, \"we need to talk about the room as seriously as the relationship.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally. A structural problem.\"\n\nWe spent the afternoon moving furniture. Aang made a low screen from cloth and light wood. Katara rearranged the shelves so medicines stayed in the proper light. Toph checked that the chairs could be moved without blocking the door. I measured the table twice and still got one dimension wrong.\n\nToph corrected me.\n\n\"You're terrible at lying to a ruler.\"\n\n\"I didn't lie.\"\n\n\"Your ruler did.\"\n\nBy evening the room had two workable areas and a sign that could be turned to say PLEASE KNOCK. It did not solve every issue, but it meant needing privacy didn't require giving a speech.\n\nKatara found me there after dinner.\n\nShe tapped on the frame.\n\n\"Can I come in?\"\n\nI turned the sign over.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nShe entered carrying two cups of tea and a small plate of dumplings. She set them down, then stood beside the table.\n\n\"I was still angry this morning,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm less angry now.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not because we drew squares.\"\n\n\"That was my favorite part.\"\n\nShe laughed softly.\n\n\"Because you listened. And because you didn't promise you'll never disappoint me again.\"\n\n\"I probably will.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\nI stood. Her hands were warm when I took them.\n\n\"I'm sorry I left you waiting,\" I said. \"I will call or send word before the plan changes next time. Not afterward.\"\n\nShe stepped closer.\n\n\"That's what I needed.\"\n\nThe kiss was quiet at first. She brushed her thumb against my cheek, and I felt the pull of affection and embarrassment together, the strange tenderness of being forgiven without being excused. When I leaned in again, she smiled against my mouth.\n\n\"Monday,\" she murmured.\n\n\"Monday.\"\n\n\"Don't make me put it on a banner.\"\n\n\"Toph would.\"\n\n\"She would spell it wrong on purpose.\"\n\nWe laughed and stayed together until the tea cooled.\n\nThe next morning, Aang added an extra square to the calendar.\n\nIt read NO SCHEDULING DURING BREAKFAST.\n\nToph changed it to NO MEETINGS BEFORE DUMPLINGS.\n\nNobody objected.\n\nIt was the first schedule we'd made that felt less like a test and more like a way to give our choices somewhere to live.","word_count":2145},"love-and-duty-48":{"number":48,"title":"Water for the Other Side","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The spreadsheet said we had enough water.\n\nThe people waiting at the east end of the bridge said otherwise.\n\nI stared at the figures and felt my face grow warm. The table I'd made showed thirty containers delivered to Riverstone Terrace, twelve to the clinic, eight to Brasswatch Yard, and six to the temporary shelter on the ridge. The total matched the supply account.\n\nIt was beautiful arithmetic.\n\nIt was a terrible distribution.\n\nKatara stood beside me, reading the paper.\n\n\"How many people are at the east shelter?\" she asked.\n\n\"Eighteen.\"\n\n\"And at the west?\"\n\n\"Seven.\"\n\n\"How many containers?\"\n\n\"Six east. Ten west.\"\n\nShe tapped the page.\n\n\"There's our problem.\"\n\nThe bridge had remained closed while the supports were repaired and tested after the storm. People could use the longer ridge route, but not every family could carry heavy containers that distance. The supply team had delivered water to the places closest to the storage depot first, then sent what remained to the harder-to-reach shelter.\n\nNobody had set out to be cruel.\n\nThe process had been convenient for the people carrying the crates, not fair to those who needed them.\n\nI reached for my pencil.\n\n\"We can fix the numbers.\"\n\n\"Eventually,\" Katara said. \"But first we need to talk to the people who are short.\"\n\nWe walked to the east shelter with Aang. The wind was still strong along the canal, and the lower path smelled of wet stone. A temporary ramp led into the hall, built after Edda's access inspection.\n\nA woman named Sela greeted us at the door.\n\n\"Do you have water?\"\n\n\"Some,\" Katara said. \"Not enough yet. We're checking what everyone needs.\"\n\nSela pointed to a row of empty vessels.\n\n\"Some of us can drink from the spring if we carry it. Others can't carry the distance. The oldest person here needs water brought to her, and the family with the baby is boiling what they can find.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Then they go first.\"\n\nI wrote down the specific needs without putting private names on the public board.\n\nAang looked at the route.\n\n\"I could carry water across the canal with bending.\"\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"You could. What happens when you leave?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"We need a system that doesn't depend on me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI felt a small rush of admiration. He'd learned to hear that question without treating it as an insult.\n\nWe returned to the square and called the distribution volunteers together. Mara had been packing supplies, Danse had helped design the carrying frames, and Greg had offered a morning shift with the handcart. Toph stood near the wall, listening to the movement through the stone floor.\n\nI put the numbers on the board.\n\n\"These are the deliveries,\" I said. \"But this is not the same as the needs.\"\n\nOne volunteer frowned.\n\n\"We delivered to every district.\"\n\n\"Yes. We didn't deliver proportionately to the people who couldn't access the other supplies.\"\n\nAnother looked embarrassed.\n\n\"We used the closest route first.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why we're changing it.\"\n\nToph tapped the floor.\n\n\"Your chart counts containers. It doesn't count the distance a person has to drag them.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nWe made a new plan with actual requirements. Katara confirmed the clinic's medical supply needs. Sela arranged a contact person at the east shelter without making the residents' private details public. Danse checked the handcart load. Edda tested the route so the deliveries wouldn't block the accessible path.\n\nAster came from Brasswatch carrying a spare lamp and a note with a corner torn away. They had been checking the backup lights near the workshop when somebody found water pooling beside the service junction.\n\n“Renn says the generator may have to stay down tonight,” Aster told us. “We need the drinking-water cart to use the north entrance. The service corridor's being kept clear for electrical inspection.”\n\nI looked at my chart. The fastest route to the east shelter ran past the very doorway Aster had named. A quicker path for water would now send a heavy cart through a place where someone could be working on wet cables.\n\n“Can the shelter wait until morning?” I asked.\n\nAmel, who had walked over with the latest request sheet, gave me a look that made my ears burn.\n\n“No. People are drinking the last of their reserve tonight.”\n\nToph tapped the paving with her heel. “Then find another route. The water isn't going to sprout legs because your chart says it's inconvenient.”\n\nAang volunteered to carry the first two containers himself. Katara stopped him long enough to ask whether somebody could do the rest without the Avatar. Beren, who was loading the cart, drew the wider path on the back of my old delivery sheet. Edda walked it with him, checked the turns, and marked where a loose crate would narrow the entrance.\n\nAster folded the new route into their pocket to show Danse before the lights failed altogether.\n\nIt meant the water would arrive later, but by a path we had actually tested. I had spent so much time making the first numbers look balanced that I had almost delivered water into an electrical hazard.\n\nWhen I crossed out the old line, the ink ran through two entire columns. I didn't bother making it neat. I wrote the new arrival time in the space below and asked Amel to read it back to me.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers for the first round, with two local volunteers learning the handling process for later shifts.\n\nNo one was assigned more than they'd agreed to do.\n\nThe revised plan meant the west shelter would receive fewer extra containers for one day. Some residents complained. They had been relieved to have a reserve and did not like hearing that it had been allocated unevenly.\n\nI couldn't blame them for feeling anxious.\n\nI could still tell the truth.\n\n\"We're not taking away your minimum needs,\" I explained. \"We're correcting a distribution that left another group without theirs.\"\n\nA man near the depot folded his arms.\n\n\"Easy for you to say.\"\n\nHe was right about one thing: I was not the person who would have to walk farther for water if the plan failed.\n\n\"Then help us test it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"We need your experience of the route. And if you find a problem, I want to know.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nWe took the draft to the east shelter before asking anyone to call it fair. A woman named Amel was distributing cups near the entrance. She held the sheet with two fingers, as though it might leave ink on her hands.\n\n\"Thirty containers,\" she read. \"For the whole terrace?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How many of those are supposed to arrive before the night shift starts?\"\n\nI checked. \"Twelve.\"\n\nShe tapped a line with her nail. \"We have six people working outside until dark. They won't be here to collect water at that hour.\"\n\nI was ready to explain the schedule, then stopped. The schedule was mine. The people waiting for water hadn't agreed to live according to it.\n\n\"What time would actually work?\" I asked.\n\nShe wrote two possible windows in the margin, then asked whether the depot could hold a few containers for people who preferred not to explain their household arrangements in public. Katara said the shelter could record quantities without publishing names. That was different from hiding how much water it had received.\n\nAang wanted to help with deliveries. Amel looked at his glider.\n\n\"Not everything can arrive from the sky,\" she said. \"Some people need to know what happens when you're not here.\"\n\nHe winced, then nodded. \"You're right.\"\n\nToph tested the stone lip of the north path with her foot. \"And the cart still has to turn a corner. Which brilliant person drew that?\"\n\nI raised my hand.\n\n\"Of course you did.\"\n\nI laughed despite the heat in my cheeks. She wasn't gentle about an error, but she didn't make me afraid to change it. There was affection in her bluntness, and I wasn't required to enjoy it every minute to know she cared.\n\nWhen the sheet finally had a plan that Amel could use, I passed her the pen. She signed the receipt for the proposed time, not for water that had yet to arrive.\n\n\"Come back when the carts do,\" she said.\n\nWe did. The plan still needed a driver's eye before it could become a real route.\n\nThe first revision nearly failed at the depot.\n\nA cart driver named Beren looked at the new sheet and shook his head.\n\n\"You've moved the east delivery earlier, but you're still using the narrow lane by the canal. My cart can't turn around there when the rain comes.\"\n\nI studied the map. He was right. In my hurry to fix the unequal water totals, I had reused the old route without checking whether the carriers could actually make the journey.\n\n\"Which path would you use?\" I asked.\n\n\"The north path. Longer, but the stones are solid.\"\n\nToph tapped her foot against the ground.\n\n\"He's right about the surface.\"\n\n\"Can Edda check the turns?\" Katara asked.\n\n\"Already did yesterday,\" Beren said. \"I watched her. She made me move a stack of crates so she could pass.\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Thank you for doing that.\"\n\n\"She didn't ask politely.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Sounds like Edda.\"\n\nWe changed the route.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers along the first run, but Sela reminded him that the local volunteers needed practice managing the handcart without bending. They divided the loads by what people could safely handle. Aang worked with them, not instead of them.\n\nAt the east shelter, a woman asked whether the delivery record would name the people who received extra water.\n\n\"No,\" Katara said. \"The public record will show how much arrived and what needs it served. Your medical or family details aren't for the notice board.\"\n\nThe woman nodded and accepted the containers.\n\nI crossed out another line on the spreadsheet. The new table looked worse than the old one. It had arrows, corrections, and a reminder to review the distribution after two days.\n\nIt was also the first version that people who used the routes had helped write.\n\nI had spent so long worrying about being a responsible member of our complicated household that I'd forgotten the same principle mattered outside our door. Caring for people wasn't making decisions for them. It was asking which decisions actually changed the day they had to live.\n\nThe second delivery went better. The east shelter received its immediate supplies before evening. The west retained enough for confirmed needs. The public ledger showed the changes and the reasons, without turning vulnerable households into a list of people to be judged.\n\nWe returned home exhausted.\n\nKatara washed the dust from her hands and looked at my spreadsheet, now covered in corrections.\n\n\"Do you still like it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. That means it's being used.\"\n\nToph sat at the table with a bowl of soup.\n\n\"Spreadsheets are like people,\" she announced. \"They get annoying when they think they're always right.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n\"Are you comparing me to a spreadsheet?\"\n\n\"Only on your worst days.\"\n\nKatara came around the table and rested a hand on my shoulder.\n\n\"You noticed a problem,\" she said. \"Then you listened. That's what matters.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"We still missed it for two days.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe didn't soften the truth.\n\nThen she kissed my forehead.\n\n\"We'll do better tomorrow.\"\n\nLater, when the dishes were done, Aang showed me the new route he'd drawn. It included shaded rest points, safe crossing times, and notes from the people who used the path daily. His handwriting was crooked from trying to fit too much information onto one page.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"It looks terrible.\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"That might be the new standard.\"\n\nHe grinned and leaned close.\n\n\"Then perhaps I can ask for something that doesn't fit in a table.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"A quiet walk with you.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\nWe went outside beneath the lanterns.\n\nThe bridge remained closed. The water was still moving. The arithmetic was still imperfect.\n\nBut the people on the other side had water, and we'd stopped confusing a balanced column with a fair day.","word_count":2050},"Love-And-Duty-05":{"number":5,"title":"No One Carries Everyone","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 5 of 40","content":"Katara was very good at noticing when everyone else needed rest.\n\nShe was terrible at noticing when she did.\n\nThe clinic stayed open three hours past sunset because a boiler explosion on the south side sent twelve injured workers through the doors. None of the wounds were life-threatening. That did not mean they were easy. Burns hurt. Fear hurt. A man who thought he might lose the use of his hand cried into his wife’s shoulder while Katara knelt beside him and told him exactly what she could heal and exactly what would take time.\n\nI spent the evening carrying water, recording names, and keeping frightened relatives from blocking the treatment tables.\n\nAang arrived twice and was sent away twice.\n\nThe first time Katara told him she was busy.\n\nThe second time I told him she would murder him if he tried to be helpful by reorganizing supplies.\n\n“She likes when I help.”\n\n“She likes when you help correctly.”\n\nHe considered this.\n\n“Fair.”\n\nToph arrived around midnight with food.\n\n“Eat,” she ordered.\n\nKatara did not look up. “In a minute.”\n\nToph set the bowl directly on Katara’s patient ledger.\n\n“Now.”\n\n“I am literally writing.”\n\n“Not anymore.”\n\nKatara’s eyes flashed.\n\nToph crossed her arms.\n\nI stepped between them with the confidence of a person who had temporarily misplaced survival instinct.\n\n“I’ll finish the ledger.”\n\nKatara looked at me. “You don’t know the treatment notes.”\n\n“You tell me. I write. Revolutionary system.”\n\nHer mouth tightened.\n\nThat was when I saw her hand shake.\n\nOnly once.\n\nFatigue, not fear.\n\nI took the pen.\n\n“Katara.”\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nNo title. No teasing. No argument.\n\n“Sit down.”\n\nFor one long second I thought she would refuse purely because I had used her own command against her.\n\nThen Toph snorted.\n\nKatara sank onto the bench.\n\n“Traitors,” she muttered.\n\nToph shoved the bowl into her hands. “You love us.”\n\nKatara looked at the stew.\n\nSomething in her face softened.\n\n“That’s the problem.”\n\nToph went still.\n\nI sat beside Katara while she ate.\n\nA young boiler apprentice appeared at the treatment-room curtain holding a folded piece of paper. The company supervisor had asked him to sign a statement saying the accident had been unavoidable.\n\nKatara read the form, then set it aside. \"Did you see what caused the valve to fail?\"\n\n\"No. I was on the other side of the room.\"\n\n\"Then you cannot certify it was unavoidable,\" she said. Her voice was tired but absolutely steady.\n\nThe apprentice looked toward the doorway where his supervisor waited. \"If I don't sign, he says they might close the shop.\"\n\nToph moved the empty food bowl away from Katara's patient ledger. \"A broken boiler isn't a personality test. Let somebody inspect the actual metal.\"\n\nAang, who had returned carrying clean blankets, hesitated. \"I can ask the council to send an inspector.\"\n\n\"Ask,\" Katara said. \"Don't promise him an outcome you can't guarantee.\"\n\nThe apprentice chose to keep the paper. Katara wrote down what injuries she had actually treated, with his permission, and kept the medical notes separate from any public report.\n\nWhen he left, she rested both hands against the counter. I wanted to catch her before she swayed. Instead I asked whether she wanted me nearby.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"And I need another healer who can make decisions without asking me every time.\"\n\nToph pushed the bowl toward her again. \"Excellent. You're delegating. Eat a spoonful to celebrate.\"\n\nAang laughed. Katara did too, despite herself. I sat close enough for her to lean toward me if she wanted, and I did not confuse that small privilege with an obligation to fix everything she cared about.\n\nThe last patient left after one in the morning. Aang returned with blankets and did not attempt to reorganize anything. He simply moved through the room extinguishing lamps while Katara checked each bed one final time.\n\n“You can go home,” she told the remaining healer.\n\nThe healer gave her a flat look. “So can you.”\n\nToph made an approving sound.\n\nKatara ignored all of us.\n\nOutside, rain had begun.\n\nAang opened an umbrella. Toph refused to stand under it because the handle was metal and she claimed she could bend it into a better umbrella if necessary.\n\nKatara walked two blocks before her pace slowed.\n\nI fell into step beside her.\n\n“You’re limping,” I said.\n\n“I’m tired.”\n\n“You’re limping because you’re tired.”\n\n“I know how legs work.”\n\n“Debatable.”\n\nShe gave me a look.\n\nThen she laughed.\n\nIt was small and exhausted.\n\nAang and Toph had moved ahead, arguing about whether Appa could fit on the clinic roof.\n\nKatara glanced at them.\n\n“They worry.”\n\n“So do you.”\n\n“I’m better at it.”\n\n“No. You’re louder.”\n\nShe bumped my shoulder.\n\nRain tapped the umbrella Aang had forced into my hand before running ahead.\n\nAfter another block, Katara said, “My mother died because she protected me.”\n\nThe sentence came without warning.\n\nI did not answer too quickly.\n\nKatara watched the wet street ahead.\n\n“I know that’s not why I do this,” she continued. “I know people like to make everything about one wound. I’m not that simple.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“But sometimes…” Her fingers tightened around the strap of her medical bag. “Sometimes if someone is hurting and I could help, leaving feels like choosing not to be there.”\n\nI understood then that her care had teeth.\n\nPeople saw warmth first. They saw healing water, speeches, hope. They did not always see the fear beneath it: the child who had lost someone because danger came and there had been nothing she could do.\n\n“You can’t be everywhere,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You can’t save everyone.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And knowing doesn’t make it feel better.”\n\nHer eyes turned toward me.\n\n“No.”\n\nWe stopped beneath the awning of a closed shop.\n\nAang and Toph had noticed and were waiting farther down the street.\n\nKatara’s face was pale with fatigue.\n\nI lifted one hand, slowly enough to give her time to move away, and touched her cheek.\n\nShe leaned into my palm.\n\nThe gesture was so immediate that my chest tightened.\n\n“You don’t have to earn rest,” I said.\n\nKatara closed her eyes.\n\nFor several breaths, rain filled the silence.\n\nWhen she opened them again, something had changed.\n\nHer gaze dropped to my mouth.\n\nMine dropped to hers.\n\nNeither of us moved closer.\n\nNot yet.\n\nKatara smiled faintly. “You’re very bossy for someone who accused me of being bossy.”\n\n“I learned from an expert.”\n\n“That sounds like flirting.”\n\n“It might be.”\n\nHer smile became real.\n\nAang called from down the street, “Do we need to walk slower?”\n\nToph shouted, “Their heartbeats say yes!”\n\nKatara groaned.\n\nI laughed into my hand.\n\n“Someday,” Katara said, “I am going to freeze Toph’s feet to the ground.”\n\n“She’ll bend the ground.”\n\n“I know. That’s why I haven’t bothered.”\n\nWe caught up with them.\n\nAang looked from Katara to me and smiled in a way that was far too knowing.\n\nToph did not say anything.\n\nThat was worse.\n\nAt their house, Katara tried to insist I did not need to stay.\n\nAang ignored her and made tea.\n\nToph claimed the couch.\n\nI found blankets.\n\nKatara stood in the middle of the room looking offended by the conspiracy.\n\n“No,” Aang said gently.\n\n“I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“You were about to.”\n\n“I was going to say I’m fine.”\n\nToph threw a cushion at her.\n\nKatara caught it without looking.\n\nAang walked over and kissed her forehead.\n\nThe tenderness between them did not make me feel excluded.\n\nThat surprised me.\n\nIt made the room feel safer.\n\nKatara looked at me over Aang’s shoulder.\n\nThere was a question in her expression.\n\nI did not know the answer yet.\n\nI only knew I wanted to stay long enough to find it.\n\nWe ended up on the floor around the low table, drinking tea too late at night. Toph leaned against one side of the couch, feet planted. Aang rested his head in Katara’s lap. Katara’s shoulder touched mine.\n\nNo one made a declaration.\n\nNo one needed to.\n\nFor that one quiet hour, care moved in every direction.\n\nAang refilled Katara’s cup.\n\nKatara tucked a blanket over Toph’s legs while pretending she had not.\n\nToph kicked a cushion under my back when she noticed me shifting.\n\nI wrote down the clinic’s final totals so Katara would not wake up worrying about them.\n\nNo one carried everyone.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nWe carried what we could, and when our arms got tired, someone else reached for the weight.\n\nI had never known love could begin to look like that before anyone had even called it love.\n\nKatara did sleep eventually.\n\nNot because any of us convinced her she deserved it. That argument only made her more stubborn. Instead, Toph sat directly in the clinic doorway and announced that nobody was allowed to bring Katara another problem unless the building was actively on fire.\n\n“What if somebody is bleeding?” a volunteer asked.\n\n“Is it a lot?”\n\n“Toph.” Katara’s warning voice could still cut stone when she was exhausted.\n\nI took the volunteer’s clipboard. “I’ve got it.”\n\nAang collected the next one. “Me too.”\n\nKatara looked between us. “You don’t know the supply system.”\n\n“Then teach us tomorrow,” I said.\n\nHer jaw tightened.\n\nFor one second I thought she would fight.\n\nThen the fight left her shoulders all at once.\n\nThat frightened me more than the argument would have.\n\nAang saw it too. He did not reach for her immediately. He simply moved the spare blanket from the shelf to the narrow cot in the back room.\n\n“You can close the curtain,” he said. “We’ll wake you if there’s an emergency.”\n\n“And define emergency correctly,” Katara muttered.\n\nToph raised a hand. “Fire larger than me.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Fine. Larger than Aang.”\n\n“No.”\n\nKatara was smiling despite herself when she disappeared behind the curtain.\n\nAn hour later, a child arrived with a split lip and a terrified father. I cleaned the cut while Aang distracted the child by making a scrap of gauze dance in tiny circles. Toph found the missing registration ledger by feeling the cabinet hinges vibrate when I slammed the wrong drawer.\n\nNone of us did Katara’s job as well as Katara.\n\nThat was never the point.\n\nWhen she woke, the clinic was still standing. People had been helped. The world had continued without requiring her to hold every edge together.\n\nShe stared at the three of us.\n\n“Do not look smug.”\n\nToph looked extraordinarily smug.\n\nWhen Katara returned to work the next day, she found the clinic ledger reorganized, badly labeled, and completely usable.\n\nShe stared at it.\n\nToph said, “You’re welcome.”\n\nKatara laughed until she had to sit down.\n","word_count":1776},"Love-And-Duty-27":{"number":27,"title":"Ice Does Not Mean Cold","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 27 of 40","content":"Katara woke me before dawn and told me to dress warmly.\n\nI had learned enough about her family to ask a better question.\n\n“How warmly?”\n\nShe considered.\n\n“Toph would call it oppressive.”\n\nI put on another layer.\n\nWe left before anyone else woke.\n\nOr rather, before anyone else admitted to being awake. As we closed the door, Toph’s voice came from the dark room behind us.\n\n“If you get eaten by a seal, I’m not helping.”\n\nKatara called back, “Go to sleep.”\n\n“I am asleep.”\n\nAang made a muffled laughing noise.\n\nOutside, the settlement was blue with early light.\n\nKatara led me past the harbor, beyond the last cluster of new buildings, toward an open stretch of snow where wind had polished the surface hard.\n\nShe carried two waterskins and a small bundle of food.\n\nI carried questions and kept them to myself.\n\nEventually we reached a flat basin surrounded by low ice ridges.\n\nKatara stopped.\n\n“This was where I practiced.”\n\nI looked around.\n\n“Waterbending?”\n\n“Bad waterbending.”\n\n“You were self-taught.”\n\n“Mostly.”\n\nShe smiled at the memory.\n\n“I used to come out here and try forms I’d invented from watching waves, or from old stories Gran Gran remembered. Half the time I froze my own boots to the ground.”\n\nI tried to imagine Katara younger. Angry. Hopeful. Desperate for a teacher who did not exist.\n\n“I wish I could have seen it.”\n\n“No, you don’t.”\n\n“I absolutely do.”\n\n“I was terrible.”\n\n“That’s why.”\n\nShe shoved me lightly.\n\nThen she stepped onto the ice.\n\nThe change in her was immediate.\n\nAt home, Katara bent with efficiency. In the clinic, water became a tool of healing. In conflict, it became extension and defense and force.\n\nHere she played.\n\nA ribbon of water rose from beneath a thin cut in the ice. She spun it around her wrist, sent it arcing overhead, split it into dozens of droplets, froze them into glittering beads, then melted them before they landed.\n\n“Show-off,” I said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe honesty made me laugh.\n\nShe bent a low wave across the ice toward my boots.\n\nI jumped back.\n\nIt stopped an inch from me.\n\nKatara grinned.\n\n“I thought this was a meaningful childhood place.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nAnother wave chased me.\n\nI ran.\n\nFor ten minutes, Master Katara, hero of the Hundred Year War, one of the greatest waterbenders alive, used her art to harass a nonbender across a frozen field.\n\nI lost badly.\n\nEventually I collapsed onto a snowbank, breathless.\n\nKatara sat beside me.\n\nSteam rose from her gloves where she warmed them with controlled bending.\n\nKatara drew a shallow line in the snow with one gloved finger. \"When the ice is this thin, you don't test it by stepping harder. You listen to the water beneath.\"\n\n\"I can't bend water.\"\n\n\"No. But you can learn not to trust a surface because it shines.\"\n\nShe handed me the end of a cord and asked me to keep both feet where the snow had been packed down. Then she raised a clear ribbon of water from the edge of the basin and let it spread across an existing crack. The ice made a low, complaining sound.\n\n\"That one's going to split when the sun warms it,\" she said.\n\n\"Wonderful. An educational death trap.\"\n\n\"An educational place to stay out of.\"\n\nI looked at her. She was grinning, which made me want to kiss her and also accuse her of trying to get me killed for a lesson.\n\nShe reached for my mitten. \"You can tell me if this isn't fun.\"\n\n\"I'm cold. I'm embarrassed about how often I fall over. And I would still rather be here than anywhere else.\"\n\n\"Three separate observations,\" she said. \"Very thorough.\"\n\nHer fingers squeezed mine through the wool. The Southern Water Tribe was more than a story about beautiful snow or tragic history. This was her knowledge, learned through cold mornings and difficult choices, and she was offering me a little of it without pretending the place had been made for my romance.\n\n\"After this,\" I said, \"I demand something hot.\"\n\n\"Tea?\"\n\n\"And maybe a kiss.\"\n\nShe glanced at the ridgeline. \"That one I can manage.\"\n\nThe promise warmed me long before her waterbending did.\n\n“Can you do that for my feet?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Cruel.”\n\n“You should have worn the other socks.”\n\n“You said these were enough.”\n\n“I said those were enough with the lined boots.”\n\nI stared at her.\n\nShe stared back.\n\n“Toph is right about you.”\n\n“Toph is never right about me.”\n\n“Statistically impossible.”\n\nKatara laughed and finally warmed the snow around my boots just enough to make the cold bearable.\n\nWe ate breakfast sitting on the ice.\n\nFor a while we talked about nothing important.\n\nThen Katara said, “People think I’m warmer than I am.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\nShe watched sunlight begin to edge over the horizon.\n\n“I don’t mean kind. I think I’m kind.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“I mean easy.”\n\nThe word settled.\n\nI waited.\n\n“They expect me to be easy to love because I take care of people.”\n\nI thought about the clinic. The speeches. Her ability to find hope when everyone else had misplaced it.\n\n“And then?”\n\n“Then I’m stubborn. Or angry. Or I remember exactly what somebody said six months ago.”\n\n“Terrifying skill.”\n\n“Or I don’t forgive something.”\n\nHer voice changed there.\n\nI looked down at my mitten—the bad-fitting one Gran Gran had given me.\n\n“Or you want things done your way.”\n\nKatara gave me a look.\n\n“I’m helping.”\n\n“You are proving the point.”\n\n“Also helping.”\n\nShe smiled despite herself.\n\nThen the smile faded.\n\n“I used to think love meant being needed.”\n\nThe confession caught me.\n\nKatara rubbed one thumb over the seam of her glove.\n\n“When Mom died, there was so much to do. Sokka needed me. The village needed everyone. Dad left to fight. Taking care of people felt like… if I was useful enough, maybe nobody else would disappear.”\n\nI did not touch her immediately.\n\nWe had spoken about my own version of that wound.\n\nNot the same history.\n\nA familiar shape.\n\n“What changed?”\n\n“Aang.”\n\nShe smiled a little.\n\n“Toph. Sokka growing up. Me getting tired.”\n\n“Excellent teacher.”\n\n“The worst.”\n\nShe exhaled.\n\n“And you.”\n\nI turned.\n\nKatara’s eyes met mine.\n\n“You let me do things for you.”\n\n“That sounds like I’m failing the lesson.”\n\n“No.” She nudged my knee. “You also say no.”\n\nI understood.\n\nCare that could be refused was different from care that created debt.\n\n“You don’t need me every time I want to be needed,” she said. “I hate it.”\n\nI laughed.\n\n“But it’s good.”\n\nThe sun broke over the ridge.\n\nLight scattered across the ice so brightly I had to narrow my eyes.\n\nKatara watched me instead of the sunrise.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That is never true.”\n\nHer mouth curved.\n\n“I love you.”\n\nThe words had been hovering around us for months in every practical gesture and furious argument.\n\nHearing them still changed the air.\n\nI stopped breathing for a second.\n\nKatara’s expression shifted.\n\nNot panic.\n\nSpace.\n\nShe did not rush to fill my silence or demand the words back.\n\nI reached for her hand.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\nThe relief that crossed her face was so open it hurt.\n\nShe kissed me on the ice.\n\nNot a first kiss.\n\nBetter.\n\nA kiss that had history behind it.\n\nArguments. Missed lunch. Threats. Soup. Snow. Her hand on my back after meetings. My fingers at her wrist when anger needed somewhere precise to go.\n\nWhen we pulled apart, my nose was cold enough to ache.\n\n“Romantic,” I said.\n\nKatara laughed against my cheek.\n\n“Want to go back?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Immediately?”\n\n“My feet are becoming spiritual concepts.”\n\nWe stood.\n\nHalfway home, Katara slipped her hand into mine.\n\nThe world around us was ice.\n\nNothing about it was empty.\n\nNothing about it was simple.\n\nIce preserved tracks. It carried weight. It cracked when pressure became too much. It melted and became water and froze again in another shape.\n\nKatara squeezed my hand.\n\nPeople mistook warmth for softness because they did not understand either.\n\nKatara could be warm and sharp.\n\nTender and unforgiving.\n\nA healer with anger in her bones.\n\nIce did not mean cold.\n\nSometimes it meant water had learned how to hold its ground.\n\nThe lesson Katara gave me on the ice had nothing to do with bending.\n\nShe showed me where to place my feet on packed snow, how to test a surface before trusting it, how to listen for the different sound ice made when water moved beneath it.\n\n“You’re teaching me earthbender skills,” I said.\n\nShe looked offended. “This is Water Tribe survival.”\n\n“Toph says standing your ground is hers.”\n\n“Toph claims ownership of gravity when it benefits her.”\n\nFair.\n\nWe crossed a frozen inlet slowly. My muscles stayed tense until Katara stopped halfway and turned toward me.\n\n“Look up.”\n\n“I’m trying not to die.”\n\n“You’re not going to die.”\n\n“That confidence is suspicious.”\n\n“Look up.”\n\nI did.\n\nThe horizon had gone pink with sunrise. Ice caught the color in long fractured bands, every crack filled with light.\n\n“Oh.”\n\nKatara smiled.\n\n“You can pay attention to danger without giving it everything.”\n\nI glanced down again.\n\n“Is that healer wisdom?”\n\n“No. That’s me telling you to stop staring at your feet.”\n\nWe continued.\n\nOn the far bank she produced two cups from her pack and warmed tea with a spiral of water heated over a small flame we had carried in a covered brazier.\n\n“Prepared,” I said.\n\n“I travel with Aang. Preparation is survival.”\n\nFrom somewhere behind us, very far away, Aang sneezed.\n\nKatara laughed so hard she nearly spilled the tea.\n\nIce held beneath us. Not forever. Long enough.\n\nOn the way back, Katara showed me how to recognize snow beginning to crust over in wind and where fresh drifts could hide uneven ground. The lesson became practical again, which seemed to relax both of us.\n\n“People romanticize the South,” she said.\n\n“I was just romanticizing the sunrise.”\n\n“That’s allowed.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nShe glanced sideways. “The cold, less so.”\n\nMy toes had gone numb despite two pairs of socks. “I would never.”\n\nKatara laughed and bent a ribbon of warmer water through a sealed skin before handing it to me.\n\n“You planned that?”\n\n“I know you.”\n\nThe words warmed more than the water did.\n\nBy the time the village roofs came back into view, I had learned three new ways not to fall through ice and one more way Katara loved: preparation so thorough it could masquerade as inevitability.\n\nAt the door, Katara knocked snow from my sleeves and said, “Next time, better boots.” I told her next time she was carrying me. She considered it far too seriously.\n","word_count":1775},"love-and-duty-45":{"number":45,"title":"Stone Does Not Vote","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"The councilor smiled at Toph as if he'd already decided what she would say.\n\n\"Master Beifong,\" he began, \"surely with your abilities you can tell us which section of the new bridge is unsafe.\"\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"I can tell you which part is cracked.\"\n\n\"Excellent. Then we'll take your word as our final finding.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe room went still.\n\nIt was a council session held at Lanternwell Square, open to the public because the bridge connected several districts and affected more than one neighborhood's budget. We sat on simple benches beneath the old lantern hooks. On the wall hung Aang's revised survey and a map showing the garden at Riverstone Terrace.\n\nThe councilor, whose name was Daron, looked momentarily confused.\n\n\"Is the damage unclear?\"\n\n\"The damage is clear. Your question is wrong.\"\n\nDaron adjusted his collar.\n\n\"I beg your pardon?\"\n\nToph put both feet flat against the stone floor.\n\n\"You want me to listen to the rock and announce a perfect answer so nobody has to ask the people who use the bridge. Stone doesn't know which carts are too wide, who needs a handrail, or how far a person can walk carrying water.\"\n\nDaron glanced toward Aang.\n\nI recognized the look. Somebody asking the most famous person in the room to translate a woman who had spoken perfectly clearly.\n\nAang folded his hands.\n\n\"I think Toph explained it well.\"\n\nI tried not to smile.\n\nDaron turned back to her.\n\n\"But your earth sense is the strongest evidence available.\"\n\n\"It's one kind of evidence.\"\n\n\"Surely it should take precedence.\"\n\nToph's smile was sharp.\n\n\"Surely you should sit down before you embarrass yourself further.\"\n\n\"Toph,\" I murmured.\n\n\"What? That was polite.\"\n\nThe chairwoman tapped her bell.\n\nWe had invited Fen, Danse, Sela, two cart drivers, and Edda, who used a wheeled chair for longer distances and had asked to speak about the bridge approach. The council had assumed they were there to observe. The assumption lasted only until Edda rose to her hands.\n\n\"I'd like the floor,\" she said.\n\nThe chairwoman looked at Daron, then nodded.\n\nEdda moved to the front with practiced patience. The table had been set too close to a bench, and someone had to shift it before she could approach.\n\n\"That's my first point,\" she said when the path was clear. \"You built a meeting room with an obstacle in the middle. Then you're going to tell me how convenient the bridge is.\"\n\nA few people laughed nervously.\n\nEdda did not.\n\n\"The handrail is too high on the north end. The ramp angle changes where the path was patched. When the stone is wet, my wheels lose traction. Master Beifong's assessment of the foundations is useful. It doesn't tell you any of that.\"\n\nToph nodded.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nDaron looked at his notes as though they had personally betrayed him.\n\nDanse stepped forward with the pressure tests from Brasswatch. He had calculated how much weight the bridge could carry under several conditions. Tamsin added a list of materials and repair hours. Fen explained how a closed bridge changed the delivery routes.\n\nSela spoke last.\n\n\"The drainage problem you're discussing isn't just about the bridge,\" she said. \"Your cart path blocks the spillway into my garden. If you redirect that water without speaking to us, you may save the bridge and flood our home.\"\n\nAang looked down at the map.\n\nHe didn't defend the old decision. He wrote the new information beside the survey.\n\nI felt something loosen in my chest.\n\nThat was what I wanted a council to look like: not a room full of people performing certainty, but one willing to admit what had been missed.\n\nA clerk at the end of the bench unfolded a second map that had not been included in the council packet. It showed the route used by the water carriers when the canal rose, with a narrow detour marked in charcoal.\n\n\"Why wasn't this with the first survey?\" I asked.\n\nThe clerk looked toward Daron. \"It was submitted after the deadline.\"\n\n\"The water doesn't read your deadlines,\" Toph said.\n\nDaron's jaw tightened. I thought he might defend the rule. Instead he asked the clerk to date the late submission and make it part of the public record.\n\nSela came forward to check whether her garden had been marked correctly. Beren pointed out that the detour was narrow enough to turn a handcart but not a loaded wagon. Toph knelt and pressed her palm against the paving stones; she could tell them where the foundations had shifted, but she did not pretend to know what a wagon could do.\n\nAang asked the carrier to explain the route without interpreting it for them. Katara wanted to know how water and medicine would reach the clinic if the bridge stayed closed during another storm.\n\nFor once I did not have to fill the room with a speech about listening. The people who crossed the bridge were doing the work of being heard. Daron wrote the new questions on the agenda, scratching out the words FINAL FINDING. The small noise of his pen against paper sounded more useful than another grand declaration of unity.\n\nDaron, to his credit, listened. After a long silence he put down his pencil.\n\n\"I've been treating Master Beifong's evidence as a way to settle the matter quickly.\"\n\nToph lifted her chin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That was unfair to the other witnesses.\"\n\n\"Also yes.\"\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n\"And to you.\"\n\nShe considered him.\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nThe chairwoman proposed a working group with specific responsibilities. Toph would assess structural movement. Edda and other bridge users would test access and safety. Danse and Tamsin would handle materials and loads. Sela and the gardeners would review drainage consequences. Fen would update the delivery plans.\n\nNobody would be expected to answer questions outside their expertise, and the bridge would remain closed until the work was safe.\n\nIt wasn't a romantic decision. It was slower, more expensive, and likely to irritate half the people in the square.\n\nIt was right.\n\nAfter the meeting, Toph marched away before anyone could congratulate her. I caught up with her beside the fountain.\n\n\"You were brilliant,\" I said.\n\nShe scowled.\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because everybody says that when I break a rock. It gets old.\"\n\n\"You didn't break anything.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nI walked beside her in silence. Her boots made soft sounds on the paving stones. She stopped near the canal.\n\n\"I wanted to,\" she admitted.\n\n\"Break something?\"\n\n\"Make a demonstration. Show him what I could do. I could've split the bad section out of the bridge in one motion and made the whole room stare.\"\n\n\"But?\"\n\n\"But the kid who brings water across it wouldn't get home any faster.\"\n\nI touched her hand.\n\n\"That's what impressed me.\"\n\nShe turned her face away.\n\n\"Gross.\"\n\n\"You're welcome.\"\n\nWe sat on the low wall, close enough that our shoulders touched. Toph rested her fingers against the stone, feeling the vibrations from carts crossing the safer street beyond the canal.\n\n\"I hate when people turn me into a tool,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"They think being good at something means they own the answer.\"\n\n\"That happens to Maui too. And Danse. And Aang.\"\n\n\"Yeah. Only I'm right more often.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nShe smiled despite herself.\n\nToph rested her palm against the wall. The stone carried footsteps from the market street, uneven and familiar. I waited for her to speak.\n\n\"There's a crack in the approach nobody mentioned,\" she said.\n\n\"Should we go back?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow. With the actual inspection crew. If I march in there now, everybody'll think I've solved it by touching a rock.\"\n\nShe sounded angry, but there was amusement underneath it too. I had learned not to mistake her confidence for an endless supply of patience.\n\n\"Do you think they'll listen when you bring it up?\"\n\n\"Maybe. They listened today when I told them a rock doesn't vote.\"\n\n\"Very eloquent.\"\n\n\"I should put it on a banner.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nShe took a breath. \"Sometimes I want to show them what I can do. Really show them. Make the whole bridge bend until nobody can pretend they know more about stone than me.\"\n\n\"That sounds satisfying.\"\n\n\"It would be. For ten seconds. Then somebody's garden floods because I moved the wrong support.\"\n\nI reached for her hand but stopped where she could feel me coming. She caught my fingers herself.\n\n\"You're allowed to be good at things without being assigned every problem,\" I said.\n\n\"Yeah. You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"Because you keep forgetting.\"\n\nShe turned toward me, the edge of a smile on her face. \"Careful, Sparky. I might start believing you.\"\n\nWe agreed to bring the crack to the paid inspection team, not to make a spectacle of it. Sela would be invited to comment before any drainage work touched her garden. Edda would review the temporary walking route. Toph wanted the findings written down in the ordinary record, not recorded as another miracle performed by a famous bender.\n\nI told her how proud I was of that.\n\n\"Don't get sentimental,\" she said.\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nShe leaned closer, paused and waited until I answered with a small movement of my own. The kiss was brief and warm. When we drew apart she pretended the canal breeze had made her blush.\n\n\"We're still getting dumplings,\" she said.\n\n\"After the inspection tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Before. I'm not negotiating on an empty stomach.\"\n\nI laughed, and she squeezed my hand. When the messenger arrived a few minutes later with Katara's letter, there was still a little flour on Toph's sleeve from the dumpling stall. It made the ordinary details of our life feel as important as anything the council had written down.\n\nA messenger approached with a small packet from the South. It was Katara's handwriting on the envelope. We opened it together because she'd addressed it to the household.\n\nThe clinic was busy. The new treatment room was functioning. She'd heard about the bridge meeting from a traveler and wanted to know whether Toph had frightened the council into competence.\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Tell her yes.\"\n\nI read the rest of the letter. Katara asked about our schedules, the repair budget, and whether we were eating properly. In the margin she had drawn a small bowl with four chopsticks.\n\n\"She misses us,\" I said.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nToph's voice softened.\n\n\"I miss her too.\"\n\nWe wrote back at the kitchen table that evening. Aang described the aerial survey. I added the council decision and the new working group. Toph dictated a paragraph about how she'd managed not to throw anybody into the canal, which she considered a tremendous personal sacrifice.\n\nWhen the ink dried, we ate dinner by the open window.\n\nAfterward Toph found me alone in the garden.\n\n\"Hey, Jasper.\"\n\n\"Hey.\"\n\nShe stood near the gate, her hands in her pockets.\n\n\"You know that Friday space on the calendar?\"\n\nI felt a smile start.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I've decided.\"\n\n\"Oh?\"\n\n\"I want it.\"\n\nI moved closer.\n\n\"All right.\"\n\n\"Not because I owe you a date.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And not because you were nice to me today.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nShe reached for my hand, found it without hesitation, and tugged me toward her.\n\n\"Because I like you, idiot.\"\n\nI laughed into the kiss. She kissed me again, softer this time, and for a moment the whole complicated house with its unfinished repairs and missing chair felt like exactly where I wanted to be.\n\nOn Friday we walked to the bridge together and checked the new ramp markings.\n\nThe bridge was still closed.\n\nToph tapped the stone beneath her feet.\n\n\"Still talking,\" she said.\n\n\"What's it saying?\"\n\n\"That we've got work to do.\"\n\nShe grinned.\n\nI squeezed her hand.\n\nFor once, neither of us was in a hurry to prove otherwise.","word_count":1955},"Love-And-Duty-06":{"number":6,"title":"What the Ground Knows","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 6 of 40","content":"Toph decided I needed self-defense lessons because I lost an argument with a crate.\n\nThe crate had been falling from a loading platform.\n\nThe following morning Toph marched me back to the same loading platform. A worker was there inspecting a frayed rope, and the entire crate had been lowered onto the ground.\n\n“See?” Toph said. “Not a mysterious attack by boxes. Somebody tied a bad knot.”\n\nThe worker looked defensive. “I didn't tie that one.”\n\n“Didn't say you did. I'm saying we're fixing the problem before Paperwork here practices being crushed again.”\n\nI wanted to be offended by the nickname. Instead I watched her find the loose end with her fingers, test the rope's tension, and call the foreman over to explain where the platform needed a second stop.\n\n“You're not teaching me to fight crates,” I said.\n\n“Congratulations. You're learning to notice when they're about to become somebody else's problem.”\n\nThe foreman promised to check the rest of the hoist. Toph asked him to put the result on the job board and tell the next shift, not just us. She wasn't asking for a medal; she wanted to know no one would stand beneath an unsafe load after we went home.\n\nWhen we reached the yard she picked up a padded staff.\n\n“Now,” she said, “about that self-defense lesson. I'm going to be right here. You can tell me to stop whenever you need.”\n\n“Does that make the next half-hour less terrifying?”\n\n“Nope.” She grinned. “But it makes it yours.”\n\nI had been standing under it.\n\nI moved in time, which I considered a victory.\n\nToph considered the entire incident a personal insult.\n\n“You froze.”\n\n“For half a second.”\n\n“That’s enough time to get flattened.”\n\n“I did not get flattened.”\n\n“Because I was there.”\n\n“You’re very humble.”\n\n“I’m the greatest earthbender in the world. Humility would be lying.”\n\nThat was how I found myself barefoot in the courtyard behind her metalbending school at dawn, regretting every choice that had led me there.\n\n“I’m not an earthbender,” I reminded her.\n\n“No kidding.”\n\n“I cannot feel the ground the way you do.”\n\n“Also noticed.”\n\n“What, exactly, are you teaching me?”\n\nToph planted one heel and folded her arms. “How to stop acting like being a nonbender means your only choices are run or wait for somebody shinier to save you.”\n\nThe words hit harder than the crate would have.\n\nI stared at her.\n\nToph’s expression did not change.\n\n“Too mean?” she asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nShe tossed me a padded practice staff.\n\nThe first lesson was not fighting.\n\nIt was balance.\n\nToph made me stand with my knees bent while she shoved my shoulders from different angles. Every time I stumbled, she called me a decorative fence post. Every time I corrected, she said nothing—which I quickly learned was praise.\n\n“Stop trying to guess where I’m going,” she said.\n\n“I’d love to. You’re hitting me.”\n\n“I’m barely touching you.”\n\n“You throw rocks for fun. Your scale is broken.”\n\nA grin flashed across her face.\n\nShe came at me again.\n\nThis time I did not try to outthink her. I felt the pressure through my feet, the change in my center, the direction of her hand. I stepped instead of locking my knees.\n\nToph’s palm slid past my shoulder.\n\n“There.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“That was good?”\n\n“Don’t ruin it.”\n\nWe worked until sweat dampened my shirt and the city began waking beyond the school walls.\n\nToph moved through the courtyard as if every inch belonged to her because, in a way, it did. She knew where I stood from the tremor of my weight. She knew when a student entered the far gate. She knew when a cart rolled down the street outside.\n\nShe also knew when I watched her.\n\n“Again,” she said.\n\n“I need water.”\n\n“You need to stop staring at my arms.”\n\nI nearly dropped the staff.\n\nToph laughed so hard she had to brace a hand on her knee.\n\n“I was not—”\n\n“Your heartbeat did the thing.”\n\n“My heartbeat is none of your business.”\n\n“It’s stomping on my business.”\n\nI threw the padded staff at her.\n\nShe caught it.\n\nOf course she did.\n\n“Violence,” she said approvingly. “We’re making progress.”\n\nI sat on the courtyard step and drank from my flask.\n\nToph dropped down beside me.\n\nFor a few minutes, we listened to the sounds of the school. Metal doors. Student voices. Someone swearing after apparently bending the wrong piece of railing.\n\n“You built this fast,” I said.\n\n“People wanted to learn.”\n\n“That isn’t what I meant.”\n\nToph tilted her head.\n\nI looked across the courtyard. “You made a place where nobody gets told their way of sensing the world is wrong.”\n\nHer face went still.\n\nI immediately worried I had stepped somewhere private.\n\nThen she picked at a chip in the stone beside her foot.\n\n“My parents had rooms for everything,” she said. “Music. Tea. Guests. Lessons. Rooms nobody used because they were supposed to look nice.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“They didn’t have a room for me to be good at anything they didn’t approve of.”\n\nThere was no self-pity in the words. Toph hated pity too much to carry it openly. There was anger, old and polished smooth from years of handling.\n\n“So you built one,” I said.\n\n“I built a lot.”\n\n“You did.”\n\nHer shoulder pressed against mine.\n\nToph's shoulder stayed beside mine for a long moment. I heard somebody sweep grit from the training yard; the broom dragged over the stones in uneven little strokes.\n\n\"Your school isn't like your parents' house,\" I said.\n\n\"Well, for one thing, people are allowed to make noise.\"\n\n\"And disagree.\"\n\n\"Especially disagree.\" She turned her head toward the worker testing the repaired rope. \"You want to know the worst thing about being treated like a porcelain doll? Everyone talks as though it's for your own good.\"\n\nI was quiet.\n\n\"Don't do that with me, Paperwork,\" she said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Act like being nice means never telling me when I'm wrong.\"\n\n\"You're wrong about my ability to catch a crate.\"\n\n\"No. You're wrong about your ability to dodge one. Different skill.\"\n\nI laughed, and she grinned at the sound. I asked whether she wanted me to hold her hand. She scoffed at the question, then offered two dusty fingers.\n\n\"Just so you stop hovering.\"\n\nShe could feel every movement through the paving stones, including the little shift when I leaned closer. The kiss I wanted was written plainly in my body. She tilted her face toward me.\n\n\"Ask,\" she said, sounding maddeningly pleased with herself.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"See? You learned something.\"\n\nI did. Her mouth was warm, her laugh arrived halfway through, and when we separated she turned toward the doorway and announced that lessons were not over. I liked her best when she was allowed to be difficult without anybody calling it a flaw.\n\nNot an accident.\n\n“Don’t get weird,” she said.\n\n“I wasn’t.”\n\n“You were about to.”\n\n“Your ability is ruining my emotional privacy.”\n\n“Your emotional privacy is loud.”\n\nI smiled into my water flask.\n\nToph’s voice changed. Softer, though she would have denied it under oath.\n\n“You don’t treat me like I’m fragile.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You also don’t do that thing where people get so scared of treating me like I’m fragile that they refuse to help with anything.”\n\nI turned toward her.\n\nToph kept her face angled toward the courtyard.\n\n“That sounds exhausting,” I said.\n\n“It is.”\n\n“What do you want me to do?”\n\n“Ask.”\n\nThe answer came immediately.\n\n“Just ask?”\n\n“Yeah. If I need an arm because we’re on ice, I’ll say yes. If I don’t, I’ll tell you to get lost.”\n\n“That second part sounds familiar.”\n\n“Good. You’re learning.”\n\nWe sat there with our shoulders touching.\n\nI thought about all the ways people tried to turn care into guessing. Guessing what someone wanted. Guessing what help meant. Guessing whether love made permission unnecessary.\n\nToph, for all her bluntness, seemed to hate guessing more than anyone.\n\n“Can I ask you something?” I said.\n\n“You just did.”\n\n“Toph.”\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Does it bother you when I’m interested in Aang?”\n\nShe did not move.\n\nMy pulse betrayed me before my courage could.\n\n“And Katara,” I added.\n\nToph’s mouth curved.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“The actual question.”\n\nI waited.\n\nShe tapped two fingers against the stone between us.\n\n“I’ve known those idiots a long time,” she said. “Aang loves like he’s opening every window in a house. Katara loves like she’s building the house around you and then getting mad when you don’t wear a coat.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nToph’s grin softened.\n\n“They’re not things I own.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“And neither are you.”\n\nMy breath caught.\n\nHer head turned toward me.\n\n“You thought I didn’t know?”\n\n“I don’t know what you know.”\n\n“I know when you lie. I know when Aang walks into a room and your heartbeat gets stupid. I know when Katara touches your arm and it gets stupider.”\n\nI covered my face.\n\n“And,” she continued mercilessly, “I know it does the same thing when I get close.”\n\nThe courtyard became very quiet.\n\nI lowered my hands.\n\nToph was smiling, but not like she had won.\n\nNot exactly.\n\n“Does that bother you?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer landed with startling force.\n\n“Does it scare you?”\n\nHer smile disappeared.\n\nThere was the vulnerable edge she usually buried under jokes.\n\n“A little,” she said.\n\nI did not rush to reassure her.\n\nThat would have made it smaller than it was.\n\n“What part?”\n\n“The part where people decide closeness means they get to decide things for me.”\n\nI nodded.\n\n“And the part where I might actually want something enough to be mad if it goes away.”\n\nThat one hurt differently.\n\nI set my hand on the stone between us, palm up.\n\nNot touching.\n\nAn offer.\n\nToph was blind. She could not see the gesture.\n\nBut the shift of my arm moved through the ground.\n\nHer hand found mine.\n\nShe laced our fingers together like it annoyed her.\n\n“You’re still terrible at stance,” she said.\n\n“I assumed this meant class was over.”\n\n“It means I’m giving you thirty seconds.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“To get sappy in your head.”\n\nI squeezed her hand.\n\nToph squeezed back.\n\nThirty seconds became a minute.\n\nNeither of us mentioned it.\n\nWhen we stood, she attacked without warning.\n\nI stepped sideways instead of freezing.\n\nHer hand missed my shoulder.\n\nToph’s grin was fierce and bright.\n\n“Again.”\n\nThis time, I was ready.\n\nThe lesson ended only when my arms started shaking badly enough that even Toph admitted the useful part was over.\n\nShe sat beside me on a sun-warmed slab of stone while I tried to recover what dignity remained.\n\n“You know,” I said, “most teachers say things like good effort.”\n\n“Most teachers are cowards.”\n\n“You could try encouragement.”\n\n“I encouraged you not to get flattened.”\n\n“That was a threat.”\n\n“Motivating, though.”\n\nI laughed, then winced when every muscle in my shoulders objected.\n\nToph’s expression shifted toward concern and immediately disguised itself as annoyance.\n\n“You’re going to be sore.”\n\n“I gathered that.”\n\n“Hot bath. Stretch. Don’t let Sugar Queen heal every ache just because she can. Your body should learn what it did.”\n\nThere was a philosophy buried inside the insult, as usual.\n\nI looked across the practice yard. “Was Aang really this bad?”\n\n“Worse.”\n\nAang, approaching with a basket of lunch, called, “I can hear you!”\n\n“I know!”\n\nHe sat on my other side. “She made me punch rocks.”\n\n“You were being evasive.”\n\n“I was twelve.”\n\n“Tiny evasive.”\n\nAang handed me a bun. “For the record, you did great.”\n\nToph made a gagging sound.\n\nI took the food. “Thank you.”\n\n“Traitor,” Toph said.\n\nBut when we stood, she slowed her stride to mine.\n\nNot enough to acknowledge it.\n\nEnough that I noticed.\n\nThe ground had a language. So did Toph. Both required paying attention to what happened underneath the obvious thing.\n\n“Again,” Toph said, and this time I laughed before I moved.\n","word_count":1992},"Love-And-Duty-20":{"number":20,"title":"What Katara Does With Anger","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 20 of 40","content":"Katara did not become less angry as she got older.\n\nShe became more precise.\n\nPeople who knew only the stories expected otherwise.\n\nThey remembered the healer. The girl who believed in people. The woman who could walk into a room full of frightened workers and make each one feel as if their pain mattered.\n\nAll true.\n\nThey sometimes forgot she had also crossed half the world to confront the man who murdered her mother.\n\nThey forgot hope and anger had always lived in her together.\n\nI was reminded at a housing hearing in late autumn.\n\nThe landlord’s name was Wen Haru.\n\nHe owned six buildings in the western district and had spent two years avoiding repairs by transferring ownership between shell companies. Roofs leaked. Furnaces failed. One stairwell had partially collapsed. Complaints disappeared in the permit office because Wen Haru’s brother-in-law supervised inspections.\n\nWe had proof.\n\nReceipts. Statements. Photographs. Medical records connecting mold exposure to respiratory illness in seventeen children.\n\nWen Haru arrived in silk.\n\nHe smiled at the council.\n\nHe called every tenant unfortunate.\n\nHe called every violation complicated.\n\nHe called every injury unforeseeable.\n\nKatara sat beside me with both hands folded.\n\nThat was the first warning.\n\nToph sat on my other side, bare feet flat against the stone floor.\n\nThat was the second.\n\nAang had recused himself from the hearing because the city charter gave the Avatar emergency mediation power and he did not want that authority to contaminate an ordinary legal proceeding.\n\nWen Haru mistook his absence for safety.\n\n“I have always cared deeply for my tenants,” he said.\n\nToph’s head turned toward me.\n\nLie.\n\nI did not need her to say it.\n\nOne of the mothers in the gallery began crying quietly.\n\nHer son had fallen through rotten floorboards two months earlier.\n\nWen Haru continued.\n\n“Unfortunately, some residents have refused reasonable access for repairs.”\n\nKatara inhaled.\n\nI put one finger against her wrist under the table.\n\nNot stop.\n\nI would never tell Katara not to be angry.\n\nOnly: choose where to put it.\n\nHer pulse hammered beneath my fingertip.\n\nThe council chair asked whether she wanted to question the witness.\n\nKatara stood.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nWen Haru smiled at her.\n\nIt disappeared within thirty seconds.\n\nShe did not shout.\n\nShe asked dates.\n\nExact ones.\n\nWhen had he been notified about the western stairwell?\n\nWho signed the repair order?\n\nWhy did the contractor invoice show payment for timber that never arrived?\n\nWhy had three families been threatened with eviction within forty-eight hours of filing health complaints?\n\nWen Haru tried charm.\n\nKatara returned to dates.\n\nHe tried confusion.\n\nKatara returned to names.\n\nHe tried outrage.\n\nKatara opened the medical file.\n\n“This child was six.”\n\nThe room changed.\n\nWen Haru stopped smiling.\n\nKatara’s voice remained level.\n\n“He fractured his pelvis because the floor of the room where he slept had rotted through.”\n\n“That was an accident.”\n\n“You were warned.”\n\n“We receive many reports—”\n\n“You were warned seven times.”\n\nSilence.\n\nKatara placed copies of each complaint on the table one by one.\n\n“Seven.”\n\nThe council chair cleared her throat. “Master Katara, the documents are already in evidence.”\n\nKatara looked at her.\n\nThe chair looked away first.\n\nI understood, suddenly, why people described waterbending as adaptable and then made the mistake of hearing gentle.\n\nWater could cradle.\n\nWater could heal.\n\nWater could also wear stone down to nothing because it had all the patience in the world.\n\nThe hearing lasted five hours.\n\nWen Haru lost his operating licenses before sunset.\n\nA receivership order transferred emergency management of the buildings to the city until repairs could be completed. His brother-in-law was suspended pending investigation.\n\nIt should have felt like victory.\n\nInstead Katara walked out of the chamber shaking.\n\nI followed her into an empty records corridor.\n\nShe pressed both palms against the wall.\n\n“Katara?”\n\n“Not yet.”\n\nI stopped.\n\nHer shoulders rose and fell.\n\nWater trembled inside the flask at her hip.\n\nNot bending exactly.\n\nResponse.\n\nAfter a minute she said, “I wanted to hurt him.”\n\nThe confession was so quiet I almost missed it.\n\nI waited.\n\n“When he called that boy an accident.” Her voice cracked. “I wanted him to be afraid.”\n\nI moved closer but did not touch her.\n\n“You didn’t hurt him.”\n\n“That doesn’t make wanting it disappear.”\n\n“No.”\n\nKatara turned.\n\nTears stood in her eyes, furious tears, not soft ones.\n\n“People act like because I chose not to take revenge once, I stopped understanding revenge.”\n\nI thought of the stories I had heard. The Southern Raiders. Her mother. A man she had found and spared not because he deserved absolution, but because Katara refused to let murder decide who she became.\n\n“I don’t think choosing differently means the feeling vanished.”\n\n“No.”\n\nShe wiped her face angrily.\n\n“I hate when people want me to be the forgiving one.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNot just Wen Haru.\n\nYears of being the person who held everyone together. Years of being praised for compassion until compassion became another expectation placed on her.\n\n“You don’t owe him forgiveness.”\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\n“You don’t owe anybody calm either.”\n\n“I owe the hearing calm.”\n\n“Maybe. Because you wanted the ruling. That’s strategy.”\n\nHer mouth tightened.\n\n“Not purity.”\n\nThe word hit.\n\nKatara’s shoulders dropped.\n\nA little.\n\nShe leaned back against the wall.\n\n“I’m so tired of people treating anger like a failure.”\n\nI sat on the floor.\n\nAfter a second, she joined me.\n\nThe records corridor was cold. Somewhere beyond the door, clerks moved boxes and argued about seals.\n\n“Toph likes anger,” I said.\n\nKatara laughed wetly. “Toph likes hitting things.”\n\n“Related field.”\n\n“She’s going to be unbearable about this.”\n\n“She already thinks you destroyed him.”\n\nKatara looked over.\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“She whispered ‘Sugar Queen kills with paperwork’ during the ruling.”\n\nKatara covered her face.\n\nHer shoulders shook.\n\nThis time with laughter.\n\nWhen we got home, Aang had made dinner.\n\nHe knew about the result. News moved fast when rich men lost buildings.\n\nHe hugged Katara in the kitchen.\n\nShe held him for a long time.\n\nThen she said into his shoulder, “I wanted to hurt him.”\n\nAang did not flinch.\n\n“I know.”\n\nToph had been sitting at the kitchen table while Aang finished cooking. She did not ask Katara to explain the whole hearing again; the tightness in Katara's steps told her enough.\n\n\"Did anybody actually fix the rotten floors?\" Toph asked.\n\n\"The ruling requires an inspection,\" Katara answered.\n\n\"That's paperwork, not a floor.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nToph turned toward Aang. \"And don't you go offering to rebuild the whole block yourself.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"You were thinking about it.\"\n\n\"Maybe a little.\"\n\nKatara made a tired sound between a laugh and a groan. She took a spoonful of stew, then looked at me across the table.\n\n\"I want to be allowed to stay angry about this,\" she said.\n\n\"You are.\"\n\n\"Even if the ruling is fair?\"\n\n\"Especially if it's only a start.\"\n\nShe ate another spoonful. Aang asked what she needed the next morning, rather than telling her he could make the whole thing easier. Katara listed two patient visits she had already promised, and then, after a pause, said she wanted an hour in which nobody asked her to be reasonable.\n\nToph tapped her spoon on the table. \"Excellent. I'll take that hour. I'm extraordinarily unreasonable.\"\n\nKatara laughed properly. I squeezed her hand only when she reached for mine. We had each chosen this complicated love knowing time and care could not be allocated by winning an argument. The kitchen smelled of ginger and rain-damp coats. Anger stayed for dinner, but it did not get to push anyone else out of their chair.\n\nKatara pulled back. “How?”\n\n“Because I know you.”\n\nNo lecture about forgiveness.\n\nNo lesson about peace.\n\nAang had learned that loving Katara meant respecting moral differences he could not solve by quoting his own values at her.\n\nHe touched his forehead to hers.\n\n“Do you want to talk about it?”\n\n“Later.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nToph entered carrying a bottle.\n\n“I brought victory alcohol.”\n\nKatara stared at it. “Where did you get that?”\n\n“Legally.”\n\nToph’s heartbeat, I was certain, did something interesting.\n\nKatara pointed toward the cabinet.\n\n“Put it away.”\n\nToph obeyed.\n\nThat was how worried she was.\n\nAfter dinner, Katara stood in the courtyard with a basin of water.\n\nShe bent slowly.\n\nNot combat forms.\n\nNot healing.\n\nOld forms from the South: circular, controlled, breath moving with the water.\n\nAang watched from the steps.\n\nToph leaned against me.\n\nI watched Katara turn anger into motion without pretending the motion erased it.\n\nWhen she finished, she came inside flushed and breathing hard.\n\nShe kissed Aang first.\n\nThen Toph.\n\nThen me.\n\nNot delicate kisses.\n\nPresent ones.\n\n“I’m still angry,” she said.\n\nToph grinned. “Good.”\n\nAang smiled. “You’re allowed.”\n\nI touched the back of her hand.\n\nKatara looked around our kitchen.\n\n“Tomorrow I’m helping the tenants organize a repair committee.”\n\nOf course she was.\n\nThat was what Katara did with anger.\n\nShe did not dissolve it into forgiveness because anger frightened other people.\n\nShe sharpened it until it could cut through lies without cutting through the person she wanted to be.\n\nThen she used what remained to build something.\n\nThat evening, Katara took me walking along the canal.\n\nShe had spent the entire day furious and useful. I had watched her turn anger into testimony, revised procedures, and a list of names that would make several officials extremely uncomfortable.\n\nNow, with the work finished, her hands shook.\n\nI did not tell her to calm down.\n\nInstead I sat beside her on the stone edge while the water moved below us.\n\n“I wanted to break his nose,” she said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I still kind of do.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nShe laughed once, bitter and tired.\n\n“People act like healing means I’m supposed to be gentle all the time.”\n\n“You froze a man to a wall once.”\n\nShe turned toward me. “Aang told you that?”\n\n“Toph. With sound effects.”\n\nThat finally got a real laugh.\n\nKatara leaned her shoulder into mine.\n\n“I don’t want anger deciding who I am,” she said.\n\n“Then don’t give it the whole job.”\n\nShe looked at me for a long moment.\n\n“Was that advice?”\n\n“Terrible, probably.”\n\n“Keep trying.”\n\nThe water moved past us, never still, never harmless, carrying light from the city in broken pieces.\n\nOn the walk home, Katara did not look calmer. She looked clearer. I was starting to understand those were not the same thing, and only one of them was actually the goal.\n\nThe water kept moving. So did she. Anger came with us, but it did not get to choose the route alone.\n","word_count":1764},"Love-And-Duty-33":{"number":33,"title":"The Night We Almost Broke","subtitle":"Love & Duty · Chapter 33 of 40","content":"The worst fight we ever had began because Aang volunteered our house.\n\nHe did not mean to.\n\nThat mattered later.\n\nIt did not matter first.\n\nA delegation from the eastern district had lost its meeting hall in the storm. Aang, trying to be helpful, told them they could use our courtyard for weekly assemblies until repairs were finished.\n\nHe told us at dinner.\n\nToph stopped chewing.\n\nKatara set down her spoon.\n\nI stared at him.\n\nAang’s smile disappeared.\n\n“What?”\n\nToph’s chair scraped back.\n\n“No.”\n\n“I thought—”\n\n“That’s the problem.”\n\nAang looked toward Katara.\n\nShe did not rescue him.\n\n“Our home is not public space,” she said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Apparently not.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“I was trying to help. Their hall is gone.”\n\n“So find them a hall,” Toph snapped.\n\n“There isn’t one available.”\n\n“That doesn’t make ours theirs.”\n\nAang looked at me.\n\nI hated that I saw the question there.\n\nNot because he expected me to agree.\n\nBecause he was suddenly counting votes in our kitchen.\n\nI stood.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis face fell.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nThe quick surrender should have ended it.\n\nInstead it made me angrier.\n\n“You don’t get to say okay like the problem is solved.”\n\nAang blinked.\n\n“I agreed.”\n\n“After you already told them yes.”\n\n“I’ll tell them no.”\n\n“And we get to be the reason.”\n\n“I won’t say that.”\n\n“You don’t have to.”\n\nKatara’s voice was low.\n\n“They’ll know.”\n\nAang stood too.\n\n“What do you want me to do? I made a mistake.”\n\nToph laughed sharply.\n\n“A mistake is forgetting milk.”\n\n“I said I’m sorry.”\n\n“You didn’t.”\n\nSilence.\n\nAang’s mouth opened.\n\nClosed.\n\nToph was right.\n\nHe had explained.\n\nHe had agreed to reverse it.\n\nHe had not apologized.\n\nAang’s frustration showed now.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nThe words came too hard.\n\nToph heard that.\n\nOf course she did.\n\n“Great apology.”\n\nKatara said, “Toph.”\n\n“No. Everybody keeps acting like because he’s trying, we have to make it easy for him when his public life swallows our private one.”\n\nAang’s face changed.\n\n“That’s not fair.”\n\nToph stood.\n\n“The courtyard was one of my conditions for moving in.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Ground access. Private house. No official Avatar residence.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Then why didn’t you remember?”\n\nAang had no answer.\n\nThat was worse than any answer.\n\nToph walked out.\n\nKatara followed her.\n\nI stayed.\n\nThat was my mistake.\n\nNot because Aang did not deserve somebody to stay.\n\nBecause I stayed for the wrong reason.\n\nThe room was unbearable. Aang looked wrecked. My instinct rose immediately: make it manageable. Translate. Soften. Solve.\n\nI began collecting bowls.\n\nAang said, “Don’t.”\n\nI froze.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“Please don’t clean because you don’t know what to say.”\n\nI set down the bowl.\n\nThat hurt because it was accurate.\n\n“I’m angry too.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No.” My voice sharpened. “You know Toph is angry. Katara is angry. I’m the one still standing here, so you’re treating me like the safer one.”\n\nAang stared.\n\nThe words kept coming.\n\n“I asked for a room with a door because I needed part of this house not to belong to your work. Toph asked for the courtyard. Katara asked for a home where public need didn’t decide everything. We wrote it down.”\n\n“I forgot.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe flinched.\n\nI hated that I wanted to take the words back.\n\nI did not.\n\nAang sank into his chair.\n\n“I don’t know how to do this.”\n\n“Neither do we.”\n\n“No, I mean…” He pressed both palms to his eyes. “Everywhere I go, people need something. If I can help and I don’t, I feel like I’m failing.”\n\nCompassion.\n\nDuty.\n\nThe traits I loved in him turning sharp inside our home.\n\n“And if helping them means volunteering us?”\n\n“I didn’t think.”\n\nToph pushed her chair back. The legs scraped across the floor, loud enough that everyone stopped talking.\n\n\"Here's the part I care about,\" she said. \"You offered a space that isn't just yours. If thirty people show up, where do my students practice? What if one of them needs the courtyard because the training floor is closed?\"\n\n\"I didn't think that far ahead,\" Aang admitted.\n\n\"No kidding.\"\n\nKatara picked up the household calendar. She had marked clinic nights in blue and the days she needed quiet in red. I could see Aang looking at all the handwriting as though he had only just understood that other people's time occupied physical space.\n\n\"We could ask the delegation what they actually need,\" I said. \"They said assembly. That doesn't necessarily mean our home every week.\"\n\nAang glanced toward the window. \"I could tell them I was wrong to offer it.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Toph said. \"You could.\"\n\nHe swallowed. \"They'll be disappointed.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\nI took a breath. I had been explicit with them about preferring polyamory and practicing ethical non-monogamy, and I loved the freedom we had created. Freedom wasn't the same thing as one person writing an invitation on a calendar that belonged to four adults.\n\n\"We negotiate our relationships,\" I said. \"Our home should get the same courtesy.\"\n\nAang nodded. He didn't reach for any of us as proof we had already forgiven him. The food was cooling. I still wanted to take his hand, but I let the disagreement be real for a while longer.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“That sounds worse every time.”\n\n“It does.”\n\nHe laughed once, miserable.\n\nI sat across from him.\n\nFor several minutes, neither of us spoke.\n\nThen Aang said, “I keep thinking the Avatar’s job is to say yes to suffering.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“If I see a need, I’m supposed to move toward it.”\n\n“That sounds noble.”\n\n“It’s killing my ability to notice what it costs other people.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNot absolution.\n\nRecognition.\n\nBefore I could answer, the front door opened.\n\nKatara came in alone.\n\n“Where’s Toph?” Aang asked.\n\n“Academy.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“She said she’s sleeping there.”\n\nThe room changed.\n\nAang stood.\n\nKatara held up one hand.\n\n“Do not go after her.”\n\n“I need to apologize.”\n\n“She told me she needs space.”\n\nAang stopped.\n\nI watched duty collide with consent in his face.\n\nHe sat back down.\n\nGood.\n\nKatara remained standing.\n\n“I’m angry at you,” she said.\n\n“I know.”\n\nShe pointed at him. “And stop saying that like knowledge is repair.”\n\nI almost laughed because she had said nearly the same thing to me months earlier.\n\nAang nodded.\n\n“You’re right.”\n\nKatara’s eyes filled.\n\n“I have spent years watching the world take pieces of you because you let it.”\n\nHe looked down.\n\n“And I love that you care. I do. But sometimes I hate the Avatar.”\n\nAang looked up sharply.\n\nKatara’s tears spilled.\n\n“Not you. The role. The thing that walks into our meals and our bed and every plan we make.”\n\nAang’s face crumpled.\n\nI stood instinctively.\n\nThen stopped.\n\nNobody needed me to interrupt this with comfort.\n\nKatara wiped her cheeks.\n\n“I hate that saying this feels selfish.”\n\n“It isn’t,” Aang whispered.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThis time the phrase meant something different.\n\nShe sat.\n\nThe three of us stayed at the table until the food went cold.\n\nNobody slept much.\n\nAt dawn, Toph still had not returned.\n\nAang went to the courtyard and stood at the threshold as if the stones themselves had become an accusation.\n\nI found him there.\n\n“I could lose all of you.”\n\nThe fear was naked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nIt was not the answer he wanted.\n\nIt was the answer our household agreement required.\n\nAny of us could leave.\n\nChoice only meant something if departure remained possible.\n\nAang closed his eyes.\n\n“I hate that.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\n“What do I do?”\n\n“You wait until Toph wants to talk.”\n\n“And then?”\n\n“You apologize without asking her to make you feel better.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“And after that?”\n\n“We figure out whether this was a break or a crack.”\n\nAang looked toward the street.\n\nFor once, he could not bend the problem.\n\nKatara could not heal it.\n\nToph could not force it into another shape.\n\nI could not file it into order.\n\nBy evening, our four-bowl table had three people sitting around it.\n\nThe fourth bowl stayed in the cupboard.\n\nNobody set it out as a symbol.\n\nThat would have been pressure too.\n\nWe ate quietly.\n\nThe house felt wrong.\n\nNot empty.\n\nUncertain.\n\nLove had survived riots, storms, public scrutiny, grief, injury, and politics.\n\nNone of those guaranteed it would survive us.\n\nThat was the night I finally understood that commitment was not the certainty that nobody would leave.\n\nIt was the willingness to change what made leaving necessary—without demanding that change purchase forgiveness.\n\nFor the next two days, we communicated like diplomats from hostile nations.\n\nKatara left notes about meals.\n\nAang answered practical questions with painful politeness.\n\nToph spent more time at the academy than necessary.\n\nI worked late and told myself it was because the charter rewrite mattered.\n\nThe house became efficient and unbearable.\n\nOn the third evening I found Aang sitting alone on the roof.\n\nI almost went back downstairs.\n\nHe heard me anyway.\n\n“You don’t have to stay.”\n\nThe sentence hurt because it was exactly what we were fighting about.\n\nI sat several feet away.\n\n“I know.”\n\nWind moved between us.\n\nAang’s voice was quiet. “I keep thinking if I can make enough room for people, then nobody has to be turned away.”\n\n“Our house is not a nation.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You keep saying that after you act like it is.”\n\nHe flinched.\n\nI wanted to take the words back because I loved him.\n\nI left them there because I loved him.\n\nAfter a while he said, “When the monks raised me, private space worked differently. People came and went. We slept in shared rooms. Guests were…” He stopped. “That doesn’t mean I get to make that your rule.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“I thought generosity was always good.”\n\n“It can still be generous. Just not with something that belongs to all of us.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nDownstairs, a door opened. Toph’s footsteps crossed the hall, then stopped directly beneath us.\n\n“She’s listening,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Stop having productive conflict without us!” Toph shouted through the roof.\n\nAang laughed unexpectedly.\n\nSo did I.\n\nThe fight was not over.\n\nBut for the first time since it began, the house sounded like ours again.\n\nBefore we went downstairs, Aang asked, “Do you want me to apologize now or after everyone’s here?”\n\n“After.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHe did. Not beautifully. Not defensively either.\n\nThat mattered more.\n\nDownstairs, the four of us finished the conversation. It took hours. Nobody won. The house felt more secure for it.\n\nNobody mistook that for failure again.\n","word_count":1754},"love-and-duty-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Longlight Kites","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"The festival committee wanted Aang to teach everybody an Air Nomad sacred ceremony.\n\nThat was how the request reached us.\n\nPip had written a polite letter asking whether the Avatar might \"share the beautiful flying traditions of his people\" at the Longlight Kite Festival. They hadn't intended harm. The committee liked bright fabrics, impressive demonstrations, and the idea that one famous person could make a local celebration feel important.\n\nAang read the letter twice.\n\nThen he set it down.\n\n\"That's not what kites are for,\" he said.\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Usually they're for flying.\"\n\nKatara hid a smile.\n\nAang looked at her.\n\n\"Don't encourage her.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nI took the letter.\n\n\"What's wrong with the request?\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"Some Air Nomad practices are meant to be taught in specific ways, by people who understand their meaning. I can show children how to make a kite. I can't turn every tradition I remember into entertainment for a festival.\"\n\nThe words were quiet, but there was grief beneath them.\n\nThe Longlight Festival belonged to Lanternweald, not to the Air Nomads. It had begun when the valley's stewards needed visible wind markers along the ridges before the winter storms. Families made bright cloth shapes and attached them to safe poles so travelers could see where the gusts were strongest. Over the years the wind markers became kites. Some people decorated them with symbols from home, but nobody had a claim on anyone else's sacred practices.\n\nThe committee had forgotten the difference between sharing a skill and borrowing a culture.\n\n\"Then we tell them,\" I said.\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"I'll teach kite-making. But not a ceremony that isn't mine to give away.\"\n\nWe went to the meeting together.\n\nPip looked mortified when Aang explained.\n\n\"I didn't mean to make a spectacle of your people,\" they said.\n\n\"I know,\" Aang answered. \"That's why I'm telling you.\"\n\nThe committee adjusted the program. The opening would explain the valley's own history of wind markers and safe travel routes. People could bring designs meaningful to them, but no one would be asked to display sacred symbols or explain private beliefs. Aang would teach balance, frame strength, and how to test a kite without endangering bystanders.\n\nTamsin approved the load limits. Fen marked the ridge where the wind was steady enough. Edda inspected the path to the viewing platform, and Danse offered to help secure the temporary railings.\n\nToph was suspicious.\n\n\"Are we doing another festival where the whole town thinks wood is indestructible?\"\n\n\"Not this time,\" I said.\n\n\"Good. My patience has limits.\"\n\nBy the next morning the square was full of frames and colored cloth.\n\nAang sat on the floor with six children and two adults, teaching them how to tie a bridle knot. He was patient when the first kite twisted sideways. He didn't take it away and fix it. He asked which part felt uneven and let the child examine the string.\n\nI thought of Lio's apprenticeship at Tideglass.\n\nThe lesson was spreading.\n\nKatara helped a family choose lighter fabric because their child couldn't hold a heavy frame for long. Toph tested the stones beneath the launching area and declared one section too loose for running. We marked it off.\n\nI made a kite with a pattern of small amber circles.\n\n\"Very original,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"It looks like a potato.\"\n\n\"That's cultural criticism, and I reject it.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nAang looked over.\n\n\"I like it.\"\n\n\"You would,\" Toph said. \"You like anything that flies.\"\n\n\"That's not true.\"\n\nShe raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"All right. Mostly true.\"\n\nThe first launch went badly.\n\nMy kite rose, tilted left, and fell into a patch of soft grass. The string tangled around my wrist.\n\nAang came toward me.\n\n\"Need help?\"\n\n\"Yes. I need you to stop looking so pleased with yourself.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"That's not a repair.\"\n\n\"Can you help with the string?\"\n\nHe sat beside me and showed me how to adjust the balance point. He didn't make the knot for me. It took three tries.\n\nWhen the kite finally rose, the cloth caught the light. The amber circles shone against the sky.\n\nI felt absurdly happy.\n\nAang watched it.\n\n\"That's beautiful,\" he said.\n\n\"It's a potato.\"\n\n\"A very graceful potato.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nPip arrived carrying two rolls of bright cloth and an invoice that was missing one line. Toph took the page, held it close to her nose out of habit, then handed it to me.\n\n\"What's missing?\" I asked.\n\n\"Someone to pay for the frame repairs,\" Pip said. \"The kite's beautiful. The carpenter still wants to eat.\"\n\nAang looked genuinely embarrassed. \"I can cover the materials I asked for.\"\n\n\"That's kind,\" Pip said. \"But we need to record it as a purchase, not a donation made by the Avatar. The next festival should know what these things cost.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Listen to the art boss.\"\n\n\"I'm the person who has to repaint the sign every time somebody invents a new title for a volunteer,\" Pip replied.\n\nThe carpenter arrived with a small bundle of bent spars. Instead of taking over, Aang showed the children how to test whether the frame pulled evenly. One girl found a crack and refused to fly her kite until it was fixed. Aang told her she was right.\n\nI watched Toph stand at the edge of the practice field, feeling the ground shudder when the larger kites tugged their anchors. She called out that a stake near the accessible route was loose. Pip moved the sign and asked the children to shift the queue, not because a celebrity had commanded it, but because people needed room to pass.\n\nAang met my eyes across the cloth and wooden spars. The pleasure in his face wasn't about being praised for a ceremony he hadn't performed. It was the ordinary delight of helping someone build something that belonged to them. I kissed his cheek when he returned, and he went pink enough for Toph to notice.\n\nAt the materials table, a boy named Lio arrived with a frame too heavy for the string he'd brought.\n\n\"I wanted it to be bigger than Maui's,\" he admitted.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Then you wanted a kite or a monument?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nKatara knelt beside the frame and asked him to lift it as he would when the wind caught. He managed it for three seconds before his arms shook.\n\n\"Would you like to make a lighter version?\" she asked.\n\nLio looked toward Maui, who had been asked to hold a line at the far end of the field.\n\n\"Will he laugh?\"\n\n\"Absolutely,\" Toph said. \"Then we'll laugh at his when it falls.\"\n\nAang sat with us and showed Lio how to trim the crossbar without weakening the center joint. He didn't take over the design. Lio chose a smaller sail, kept the original painted symbol, and wrote down the dimensions in his notebook so he could teach the next person.\n\nWhen the kite finally lifted, Maui cheered so loudly that two others looked around.\n\nLio rolled his eyes. Then he grinned.\n\nI thought about the apprentices at Brasswatch and the first boats at Tideglass. Skill was traveling between the districts without requiring everybody to become the same sort of person.\n\nA volunteer came by to ask whether the event would run another hour. The sky was still bright, but Fen's wind board showed stronger gusts expected near dusk.\n\nAang checked the reading and shook his head.\n\n\"We close launches at the posted time. People can stay for food and music if they like.\"\n\nHe looked disappointed. He loved seeing the children fly, and I knew how much the afternoon meant to him.\n\n\"Are you okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said, then corrected himself. \"I'm sad to stop. That doesn't mean the rule is wrong.\"\n\nToph made a satisfied noise.\n\n\"Finally. An Avatar who understands weather isn't impressed by fame.\"\n\nHe stuck his tongue out at her.\n\nI laughed, then helped roll the unused string for the next festival.\n\nThe festival continued through the afternoon. Some kites never flew. Some tore in the gusts. One child painted a fierce red bird that spun in circles before landing directly on Maui's head.\n\nHe declared it an act of war.\n\nNobody believed him.\n\nA gust snapped one of the western marker lines. The kite spun sideways, its cord whipping against the post.\n\n“Everybody back,” Katara called, moving the children behind the safety rope.\n\nToph planted both feet and felt the post through the stone. “Anchor's loose. Not the whole ridge. Stop panicking about the whole ridge.”\n\nAang could have caught the kite in a column of air. He looked at the children under the line and decided against it.\n\n“We let it come down,” he said. “Nobody stands under a snapping cord for a show.”\n\nPip asked whether they should cancel the festival. Katara asked for the wind readings and the injury report. Toph asked who had inspected the anchor that morning. Aang listened without treating his title as a substitute for answers.\n\n“Close the west side,” he said. “The stewards can decide if the east markers stay up.”\n\n“And if they don't?” I asked.\n\n“Then everybody gets very good at folding kites.”\n\nWe recovered the fabric after the post was secured. Nobody had to be the hero of the incident; someone had missed a weak anchor, and others had noticed in time.\n\n“Would the Air Nomads have laughed at the potato kite?” I asked.\n\n“Absolutely,” Aang said, and his smile carried more than one feeling. For a moment I could hear delight and loss together, without trying to make either one disappear.\n\nNear sunset, Aang went to the edge of the ridge alone. I followed, leaving enough space for him to turn back if he wanted quiet.\n\n\"Mind company?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\nThe sky was pale gold. Kites moved above the valley like small living shapes, each with a different line and angle. The old Threadway arches glowed below us, not as trophies but as paths people could choose.\n\n\"I miss them,\" Aang said.\n\nI didn't need to ask who.\n\nHe looked at the kites.\n\n\"When people ask me to perform the traditions of the Air Nomads, part of me wants to say yes. I want them to know how beautiful my people were. I want the world to remember.\"\n\n\"And the other part?\"\n\n\"Doesn't want their memory reduced to a show.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You get to decide what you share.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I feel selfish.\"\n\n\"Preserving meaning isn't selfish.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You sound like Katara.\"\n\n\"High compliment.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe stood together while the first evening lanterns came on.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you ever feel pulled between everyone you love?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How do you know where you belong?\"\n\nI thought about the calendar at Riverstone, the quiet nights at Jasmine Steps, the workshop lights at Brasswatch, and the sea-glass orchard at Tideglass. I thought of Katara coming home, Toph choosing her own time, and Aang standing beside me with a history I could respect but never replace.\n\n\"I don't think belonging is a single place,\" I said. \"I think it's the choices we keep making honestly.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\n\"That's a good answer.\"\n\n\"Don't let Toph hear you.\"\n\n\"She'll say it's a terrible answer.\"\n\n\"Then she'll steal it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him under the first star, gentle and unhurried. The wind tugged at my hair and carried the sound of the festival up the ridge.\n\nWhen the wind calmed, the committee discovered that the largest kite had torn its lower seam. One of the volunteers suggested keeping it on display with Aang's name painted across the frame, as though the Avatar had made it himself.\n\nAang looked appalled. “I didn't.”\n\n“We thought it would raise donations,” the volunteer said.\n\nToph ran two fingers along the frame. “Then put the maker's name on it. Unless the maker is a mysterious enchanted knot with a celebrity agent.”\n\nPip checked the lending list. The kite had been built by two young apprentices using canvas borrowed from a Tideglass sail shop. One of them had agreed to a public demonstration, not to surrender their work or be replaced by a famous signature.\n\nKatara offered to help write an accurate sign explaining who had constructed it and why the flying test stopped when the cord began to fray. The volunteer looked disappointed. An almost-spectacular failure was not as marketable as a legend about the Avatar.\n\nAang knelt beside the damaged seam. “Would the makers like help repairing it?”\n\n“Ask them,” Pip replied.\n\nHe did. One wanted to work with him; the other preferred Toph's advice about the frame. Toph made an exaggerated show of being honored and then spent twenty minutes listening while the apprentice explained their own design.\n\nI watched Aang carry the repair box without being asked to bless anything. He looked happier when the second child managed the knot without him than he had when the crowd had shouted his name.\n\nThe festival belonged to the valley. The Air Nomad stories he chose to share still belonged to his people. Neither fact diminished the other.\n\nLater we packed away the kites that could be reused and labeled the damaged ones for repair. Aang helped without being asked to perform anything sacred.\n\nAt home, Katara had made tea. Toph was complaining about the potato kite.\n\nI hung it by the window.\n\nIt looked ridiculous.\n\nIt was mine.","word_count":2264}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="love-and-duty");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=83842;})();
